MEAFORD,escribe,https://meaford.civicweb.net/document/187422/2025-28%20-%20To%20Establish%20the%20Heritage%20Advisory%20Committee%20(Updated%20Terms%20of%20Reference).pdf,"Report № DEV2025-12 - Addendum 
 
Page 1 of 1 
 
 
DEV2025-12 – Establishing an Updated Terms of Reference 
for the Heritage Advisory Committee - Addendum 
Date: 
Monday, May 12, 2025 
From: 
Denise McCarl, Manager, Planning Services 
Roll № 
N/a 
Recommendation 
Be it resolved that By-law 2025-28, being a by-law to establish a Heritage 
Advisory Committee, which includes an updated Terms of Reference for the 
Committee, be taken as read a first, second and third time and finally 
passed. 
Background 
Report DEV2025-12 was considered by Council on Monday, April 28, 2025.  
This addendum report provides a brief summary of updates made based on 
discussion and direction at Committee of the Whole. That direction was to 
ensure the draft Terms of include a provision that the Chair position will be 
held by the Council member appointed to the Committee.  
Analysis 
Staff reviewed the draft Terms of Reference and confirmed the requirement 
for the Chair to be a member of Council was not part of the draft document. 
Staff added an additional clause in the definition of Chair to provide that 
clarity in section B.3.  

 
 
By-law 2025-28 
 
Page 1 of 1 
The Corporation of the Municipality of Meaford 
By-law Number 2025-28 
Being a by-law to establish the Heritage Advisory 
Committee 
Whereas, Section 5 (3) of the Municipal Act, S.O. 2001, c.25 provides that 
municipal power shall be exercised by by-law; and 
Whereas, Section 28(1) of The Ontario Heritage Act R.S.O. 1990, C.O.18 as 
amended provides that a municipality may establish a municipal heritage 
committee to provide advice on Parts IV and V of the act and other heritage 
matters as specified through by-law; and 
Whereas, the Council of the Corporation of the Municipality of Meaford 
deems it expedient and necessary to establish the Heritage Advisory 
Committee acting as the municipal heritage committee under Section 28 of 
the Ontario Heritage Act. 
The Council of the Corporation of the Municipality of Meaford enacts 
as follows: 
1. 
That the Municipality of Meaford Heritage Advisory Committee is 
hereby established. 
2. 
That the Heritage Advisory Committee Terms of Reference, attached 
hereto as Schedule A and forming part of this by-law, are hereby 
adopted. 
3. 
That By-law 2019-33 is hereby repealed. 
4. 
That this by-law shall come into force and take effect upon being 
passed by Council. 
Read a first, second and third time and finally passed this 12th day 
of May, 2025. 
 
Ross Kentner, Mayor 
 
Margaret Wilton-Siegel, Acting 
Clerk 
 

 
Heritage Advisory Committee Terms of Reference 
Page 1 of 8 
Terms of Reference 
Heritage Advisory Committee 
A. Mission Statement 
The Meaford Heritage Advisory Committee is established by Council for the 
purpose of making recommendations to Council on the identification, 
conservation and preservation of buildings and landscapes of cultural 
heritage value or interest, pursuant to the Ontario Heritage Act.  
A. 
Objectives 
Recognizing that heritage is an important part of the fabric of the 
Municipality of Meaford, the objectives of the Meaford Heritage Advisory 
Committee include the following: 
• To foster community pride within the Municipality 
• To attract tourism to the Municipality 
• To support economic growth through the preservation of Meaford’s 
Heritage District 
B. General 
1. 
The Meaford Heritage Advisory Committee (HAC) shall be created by 
Council By-law which will include these Terms of Reference (TOR). 
2. 
The HAC shall consist of up to seven voting members appointed by 
Resolution of Council. 
3. 
The membership shall contain at least one member of Council, who 
shall serve as the committee chair, and may contain two members 
at the discretion of Council.  
4. 
Where possible, a youth representative may be appointed. 

 
Heritage Advisory Committee Terms of Reference 
Page 2 of 8 
5. 
The members of the HAC shall be appointed in accordance with the 
Municipality’s Appointments Policy. 
6. 
Members of the HAC will be selected based on a demonstrated 
interest in heritage, local knowledge and/or relevant expertise. The 
selection process will be open and merit-based, with consideration 
given to ensuring diversity and inclusion. 
7. 
No public member of HAC shall be a member of another municipal 
committee or Board. 
8. 
The Mayor shall be an ex-officio member of the HAC. 
9. 
The term of office of the members of the HAC shall expire as of 
January 31 immediately following each regular municipal election.  
C. Definitions 
1. 
“Chair” means the person chairing any meeting of the HAC. 
2. 
“Committee” means the Municipality of Meaford Heritage Advisory 
Committee (HAC). 
3. 
“Council” means the Mayor and Council of the Municipality of 
Meaford. 
4. 
“Deputation” means a verbal presentation about a subject that is on 
the published HAC agenda for that meeting. 
5. 
“ex-officio” means a member that may fully participate in HAC 
discussions but cannot vote. 
6. 
“Majority” shall mean more than half of the Members present and 
voting on an issue. The count shall include only those present, 
including the Chair of the Meeting. 
7. 
“Member” shall mean a person appointed by Municipal Council 
Resolution to the HAC and does not include an ex-officio member. 
8. 
“Municipality” shall mean the Municipality of Meaford. 
9. 
“Non-statutory Role” shall mean activities the members of the 
Heritage Committee engage in which are not set out in the Ontario 

 
Heritage Advisory Committee Terms of Reference 
Page 3 of 8 
Heritage Act, and may include performing research and advising on 
by-laws, programs and policies related to heritage matters.   
10. 
“Presentation” means the verbal and/or visual provision of 
information to Council by an individual, community group or 
organization. 
11. 
“Procedural By-Law” shall mean the most current By-Law concerning 
the Proceedings of Council and its Committees as adopted by 
Council. 
12. 
“Public Works” encompass engineering, construction and related 
activities carried out by government for the benefit of citizens, and 
include the acquisition, leasing, maintenance and disposal of 
immovable assets held by the municipality. 
13. 
“Quorum” shall be the number equal to a majority of members of 
the whole Committee. (i.e. a quorum is 4 in the case of a 7-member 
HAC). 
14. 
“Statutory Role” shall mean the work of the Committee in advising 
Council on Heritage matters as set out in the Ontario Heritage Act. 
15. 
“Vice-Chair” shall mean the person who has been selected to the 
position by a majority of the HAC and shall have all the powers of 
the Chair in the absence of the Chair. The Vice-Chair shall be elected 
from among the members at the inaugural meeting of the HAC then 
at the first meeting of each year thereafter. 
D. Mandate 
The Heritage Advisory Committee has a Statutory Role under the Ontario 
Heritage Act, 1990, to provide advice and recommendations to Council. The 
Heritage Advisory Committee may also perform a Non-statutory Role, under 
the direction of Council.  
Statutory Role of the Committee  
The Ontario Heritage Act (s. 28) defines the committee's statutory role is 
to:  

 
Heritage Advisory Committee Terms of Reference 
Page 4 of 8 
• Advise and assist the council on all matters relating to the 
designation of individual properties under Part IV or heritage 
conservation districts under Part V 
• Make recommendations on applications to alter, erect, demolish or 
remove any buildings or structures on designated properties 
• Advise Council on applications to repeal by-laws which designate 
individual properties as heritage properties 
• Make recommendations to Council whether to enter into heritage 
conservation easement agreements or covenants, and  
• Advise and assist Council on other heritage matters as Council may 
deem appropriate by by-law. 
Non-statutory Role 
The Heritage Advisory Committee may engage in non-statutory activities 
with the aim of heritage preservation, public engagement and awareness 
and heritage recognition in the community, including: 
• Researching, identifying and evaluating all the properties and areas 
that may deserve protection by maintaining an inventory of listed 
heritage properties (structures) 
• Identifying issues related to designated or listed properties under 
the Ontario Heritage Act 
• Promoting heritage conservation as well as advising property owners 
on appropriate conservation and maintenance practices 
• The development of community recognition for architectural 
preservation through commemorative initiatives such as plaque 
programs, awards for restoration and sympathetic infill development 
• Advising Council of new heritage legislation and funding initiatives  
• Advising Council on changes to by-laws and policies which may 
impact Heritage matters, such as Official Plan policy, Zoning By-law 
amendments, Property Standards By-laws and so on. 

 
Heritage Advisory Committee Terms of Reference 
Page 5 of 8 
E. Meetings 
1. 
Frequency: Scheduled meetings will be held six times per year, with 
additional meetings called as necessary by the Chair and CAO acting 
collaboratively. 
2. 
Quorum: At least 50% plus one (1) of the appointed members must 
be present to constitute a quorum. 
3. 
Agendas: Shall be prepared and published by municipal staff. A 
meeting between a member of the Legislative Services division, the 
staff subject matter expert, committee Chair and a rotating member 
of the committee shall take place a week prior to agenda 
publication. 
4. 
Minutes: To be taken at each meeting and a draft will be circulated 
to members within a week after the meeting. 
F. Administration 
1. 
Members of the HAC shall sign the Municipality’s Advisory 
Committee & Volunteer Code of Conduct and are subject to 
investigation by the Municipality’s Integrity Commissioner following 
alleged breaches of the Code, or the Municipal Conflict of Interest 
Act.  
2. 
In the case of a disagreement between these Terms of Reference 
and the Municipality’s Procedural By-Law, the Procedural By-Law 
shall govern. 
3. 
Council, on the recommendation of the HAC, may declare vacant, 
the seat of any HAC member missing from three consecutive regular 
meetings without a valid reason. 
4. 
All meetings of the HAC, whether Regular or Special, shall be open 
to the public, and shall be live-streamed.  
5. 
Presentations 
a. 
A person or group wishing to make a Presentation to the 
committee shall provide the Committee Coordinator or 

 
Heritage Advisory Committee Terms of Reference 
Page 6 of 8 
designate with written notice no later than 14 days prior to the 
meeting. Such request shall state the specific nature of the 
matter to be presented. The presentation must be provided 
seven days prior to the meeting for inclusion in the package. 
b. 
Presentations shall be limited to 10 minutes. The duration may 
be extended at the discretion of the Chair.  
c. 
Presenters may only present once every 12 months on the 
same topic. 
d. 
The Chair and Clerk shall have the discretionary authority to 
determine whether a deputation is within the mandate of the 
committee and if sufficient detail has been provided in 
preparation for the subject delegation. 
e. 
The Clerk shall, having regard to time sensitive issues, have 
the authority to determine the meeting at which a 
presentation will be scheduled. 
6. 
Deputations 
a. 
Deputations shall be limited to five minutes.  
b. 
Deputations shall register in advance by contacting the 
Committee Coordinator or designate, or by filling out the 
request form at the meeting. 
c. 
Deputations may only be about an item listed on the meeting 
agenda and shall not be accompanied by any presentation 
materials. 
d. 
The subject matter of the deputation must be a matter within 
the Committee’s area of responsibility as defined in Section D 
– Mandate. 
e. 
The Chair and Clerk shall have the discretionary authority to 
determine whether a deputation is within the mandate of the 
committee and if sufficient detail has been provided in 
preparation for the subject delegation. 
7. 
No subcommittees of the HAC shall be formed, except as explicitly 
approved through Council resolution. Working groups may be 

 
Heritage Advisory Committee Terms of Reference 
Page 7 of 8 
formed of no more than three committee members, to engage in 
non-statutory activities, such as research on properties, policies, 
programs and tools, by resolution of the Committee. Working groups 
shall report back to the Committee on their work and 
recommendations.  
G. Roles 
1. 
The role of the Chair is to: 
a. 
Chair meetings, ensuring that proceedings are held, and 
recommendations and decisions made, in accordance with 
applicable legislation and municipal procedures. 
b. 
Provide a verbal report of each meeting to Council at the 
Council meeting immediately following a HAC meeting. 
c. 
Act as the spokesperson for the committee where necessary. 
d. 
Develop committee agendas in consultation with municipal 
staff and committee members. 
2. 
The role of committee members is to: 
a. 
Attend all scheduled meetings. 
b. 
Read and understand all information supplied to them. 
c. 
Undertake training as required to effectively perform their role 
as a committee member. 
d. 
Conduct research on request and report back to the 
committee. 
e. 
Be familiar with municipal, provincial and federal legislation 
that applies to heritage conservation, protection and 
preservation. 
f. 
Publicly support the recommendations of the HAC. 
3. 
The role of the staff resource(s) is to: 
a. 
Acting as a resource for the Committee during meetings that 
may include presenting background reports, distribution of 

 
Heritage Advisory Committee Terms of Reference 
Page 8 of 8 
government information and new legislation pertaining to the 
mandate of the HAC, etc. 
b. 
Facilitate the recommendations of the HAC to Council through 
the creation of reports. 
4. 
The role of Legislative Services staff is to: 
a. 
Develop an annual calendar of meetings. 
b. 
Develop committee agendas in consultation with the Chair and 
committee members. 
c. 
Publish and distribute committee agendas, including 
associated documentation. 
d. 
Take minutes at committee meetings. 
e. 
Publish and distribute minutes following committee meetings. 
5. 
Additional municipal staff shall be in attendance at HAC meetings as 
required to provide reports and advice to the Committee. 
H. Reporting 
1. 
The committee Chair shall provide a verbal report to Council at the 
Council meeting immediately following a HAC meeting. 
2. 
Recommendations to Council from HAC may be presented by: 
a. 
Listing the recommendation on a Council agenda under the 
“Updates from Councillors on Committees” section; or 
b. 
Including the recommendation in a staff report to Council. 
3. 
The Chair and CAO shall have the discretionary authority to 
determine how recommendations from the HAC are brought forward 
to Council.  
4. 
Draft HAC Minutes shall be posted on the municipal website as soon 
as practical following each meeting.",pdf,2026-11-23
