MONTAGUE,civicweb,https://montague.civicweb.net/FileStorage/9F9AA618A8DF4CB6BE8C180AB4624D3A-Proposal%20to%20Expand%20Strong%20Mayor%20Powers.pdf,"REPORT 
 
 
  ACTION  
 
  INFORMATION 
 
TO: 
 
 
Reeve Jennings & Members of Council 
FROM: 
 
Municipal Manager/Clerk, K. Cote 
DATE: 
 
April 15th, 2025 
 
 
RE: 
Proposal to Expand Strong Mayor Powers 
 
 
Recommendation:  
 
THAT Council direct Staff to provide comments to the Ministry of Municipal Affairs and 
Housing advising of Montague Township Council’s position on the proposal to expand 
Strong Mayor Powers and Duties to Additional Municipalities, as proposed in Ontario’s 
Regulatory Registry under Proposal Number 25-MMAH002. 
 
Background: 
 
The introduction of the Strong Mayors, Building Homes Act, 2022, resulted in 47 Ontario 
Municipalities being designated as having strong mayor powers.  These powers are 
assigned to the Head of Council. 
 
The purpose of designating certain municipalities as having strong mayor powers is to 
facilitate the advancement of certain provincial priorities including: 
 
• 
building 1.5 million new homes by December 31st, 2031 
• 
constructing and maintaining infrastructure to support housing, including: 
o 
transit 
o 
roads 
o 
utilities 
o 
servicing 
 
A Head of Council has the following powers and duties: 
 
• 
The Head of Council can choose to appoint their municipality’s chief administrative 
officer. 
 
• 
The Head of Council can hire certain municipal division heads ⁠— excluding statutory 
positions.  Positions that are excluded from this power include: 
 
o 
the clerk or deputy clerk 
o 
a treasurer or deputy treasurer 
o 
an Integrity Commissioner 
o 
an Ombudsman 
o 
an Auditor General 
o 
a registrar, as described in the Municipal Act, 2001 

o 
a chief building official, as defined in the Building Code Act, 1992 
o 
a chief of police, as defined in the Police Services Act, R.S.O. 1990 
o 
a fire chief, as defined in the Fire Protection and Prevention Act, 1997 
o 
a medical officer of health, as defined in the Health Protection and Promotion 
Act, R.S.O. 1990 
o 
other officers or heads of divisions required to be appointed under the 
Municipal Act, 2001, City of Toronto Act, 2006, or any other Act 
o 
any other persons identified in O. Reg 530/22 
 
• 
The Head of Council can choose to create and re-organize the structure of the 
municipality. 
 
• 
The Head of Council can create new committees of council made under the 
Municipal Act, 2001, and City of Toronto Act, 2006, where all members are council 
members, and assign their functions.  The Head of Council is also able to appoint 
the chairs and vice-chairs of such committees of council. 
 
• 
The Head of Council can bring forward matters for council consideration if they are 
of the opinion that considering the matter could potentially advance a provincial 
priority. 
 
• 
The Head of Council can propose certain municipal by-laws if they are of the opinion 
that the proposed by-law could potentially advance a prescribed provincial priority.  
When using this power, the Head of Council can only propose by-laws made under: 
 
o 
The Municipal Act, 2001 
o 
The City of Toronto Act, 2006 
o 
The Planning Act, R.S.O. 1990 
o 
 Section 2 of the Development Charges Act, 1997 
 
• 
The Head of Council can choose to veto certain by-laws if they are of the opinion 
that all or part of the by-law could potentially interfere with a provincial priority.  Only 
by-laws approved by council made under the Municipal Act, 2001, the City of Toronto 
Act, 2006, the Planning Act, R.S.O. 1990, and section 2 of the Development Charges 
Act, 1997, may be vetoed by the head of council. 
 
o 
If the head of council uses their veto power, council then has the ability to 
override the veto.  Within 21 days after the clerk provides the written veto 
document to the members of council, council may override the veto if two-thirds 
of all council members vote to override the veto.  During this process, the Head 
of Council remains as a member of council for council decision-making with 
one vote. 
 
• 
The Head of Council can direct staff to do certain things related to their additional 
powers and duties.  Direction from the Head of Council to staff under this authority 
must be provided in writing.  For example, the Head of Council could direct staff to 
undertake research and provide advice on policies and programs or to implement 
any decisions related to their additional powers and duties. 
 

• 
The Head of Council may delegate certain strong mayor powers to council.  These 
include the following: 
 
o 
appointing a chief administrative officer 
o 
creating, assigning functions and appointing chairs of committees of council 
 
• 
The Head of Council may also delegate certain strong mayor powers to either council 
or the municipality's chief administrative officer (if one is appointed).  These include 
the following: 
 
o 
hiring certain municipal division heads 
o 
changing the organizational structure of a municipality 
 
• 
The Head of Council is required to propose the budget for the municipality each year 
by February 1st. The Head of Council must share the proposed budget with each 
member of council and the municipal clerk and make it available to the public.  If the 
Head of Council does not propose the budget by February 1st, council must prepare 
and adopt the budget. 
 
• 
The Head of Council is also able to initiate and prepare in-year budget amendments 
to raise additional amounts from property tax.  The Head of Council may propose 
the budget amendment by sharing the proposed budget amendment with each 
member of council and the municipal clerk and making it available to the public. 
 
Discussion & Options: 
 
On April 9th, 2025, the Province announced proposed amendments to O. Reg 530/22 of 
the Municipal Act, 2001, to expand the list of municipalities with strong mayor powers.  
Currently there are 47 Ontario municipalities with strong mayor powers.  The proposed 
list would include an additional 169 municipalities.  The municipalities proposed to be 
added to this list include those with a council composition of six (6) or more members and 
this would take effect as of May 1st, 2025. 
 
The posting date of this proposed amendment was April 9th, 2025, with a commenting 
period ending April 16th, 2025 – only 7 days. 
 
The impact of this amendment would depend on how a Head of Council in a designated 
municipality chooses to use these strong mayor powers and how the municipality would 
support the implementation of these powers. 
 
Financial Implications: 
 
Potential financial implications are unknown at this time.  While the Township of Montague 
is not noted on the proposed list of municipalities, there is always potential for financial 
impact when neighbouring municipalities have strong mayor powers. 
 
Attachments: 
 
None.",pdf,2026-11-23
