Summer, 2026
The Ennismore Community 
Improvement Plan Public Realm 
Project at Tara and Ennis Roads 
was completed in 2025, revitalizing 
the village core and improving 
pedestrian connectivity.
Delivered through a partnership 
between the County of Peterborough 
and Selwyn Township, the project 
included road reconstruction, 
drainage upgrades, new sidewalks 
along Ennis Road to Earl Avenue, 
curbs, and asphalt paving to enhance 
safety and accessibility.
Streetscape improvements such 
as decorative lighting, coloured 
concrete crosswalks, landscaping, 
benches, railings, and new dry stack 
stone wall features have added 
character and vibrancy to the area.
Completed just in time for Ennismore 
Heritage Days on July 12, 2025, the 
renewed intersection welcomed 
residents and visitors as part of the 
community celebration.
Core of Ennismore Transformed 
Just in Time for Heritage Celebrations
Ennismore Inclusive 
Playground Project 
Nears Completion
Mayor Connects with 
Students During Local 
Government Week 2025
The Ennismore Inclusive Playground 
Project at the Ennismore Waterfront 
Park is nearing completion, creating 
a more accessible and inclusive 
outdoor space for all. Led by Jude’s 
Joy, and the Ennismore Optimist 
Club, the project has been supported 
by community fundraising and a 
$145,300 grant from the Ontario 
Trillium Foundation.
What began as a barrier-free 
playground has expanded to include 
inclusive play equipment, accessible 
pathways, improved access from 
the parking lot, and connections 
to the beach. New waterfront 
features include a mobi-mat for 
beach access and an accessible sand 
table for sensory play. Additional 
amenities such as buddy benches 
and accessible picnic tables will help 
ensure the space is welcoming and 
comfortable for everyone.
To learn more, visit: engage.
ptbocounty.ca/ennismore-inclusive-
playground-project.
As part of Local Government Week 
2025, Mayor Sherry Senis visited local 
schools to connect with students 
and share insights about municipal 
government and community 
leadership.
During the visits, students had 
the opportunity to ask thoughtful 
questions, share ideas, and learn more 
about how municipal Council works 
to support and bring community 
initiatives to life. The engaging 
discussions offered students a firsthand 
look at local government and the 
important role it plays in shaping their 
community.
The Township extends its thanks to the 
teachers and students for their warm 
welcomes, enthusiasm, and meaningful 
conversations throughout the week.
