LAND ACKNOWLEDGEMENT
 3.2
NATIONAL ANTHEM
 3.3
OPENING REMARKS - MAYOR AND CAO
 3.4
ADDITIONS/DELETIONS TO AGENDA
 3.5
ADOPTION OF MINUTES
 1.
Regular Council Meetings of June 9 and June 16, 2026.
 3.6
CALL UPON THE CITY CLERK TO REVIEW COMMITTEE-OF-THE-WHOLE (IN-CAMERA) ITEMS TO BE ADDED TO THE AGENDA BLOCK
 3.7
DISCLOSURES OF INTEREST
 3.8
COUNCILLORS TO DETERMINE AGENDA ITEMS AND BY-LAWS TO BE REMOVED FROM THE BLOCK FOR DISCUSSION IN COMMITTEE-OF-THE-WHOLE (OPEN)
 4.
ORAL REPORTS AND DELEGATIONS
 4.1
LEGISLATED PUBLIC HEARINGS/MEETINGS - NIL
 4.2
DELEGATIONS- (10 minutes maximum per delegation) 
 1.
John Brinkman, Coyle Creek resident re: Coyle Creek Stormwater Management Facility.  Ref. No. 24-78
 RECOMMENDATION:
THAT Council receives for information the delegation regarding the Coyle Creek Stormwater Management Facility.
4.3
PRESENTATIONS 
 1.
Dave Augustyn, Facilitator re: Coyle Creek Stormwater Management Facility - Community Engagement Overview.   Ref. No. 24-78
 2.
Sherri-Marie Millar, Director of Infrastructure Services & City Engineer and Kelly Walsh, Manager of Capital Engineering re: Coyle Creek Stormwater Management Facility.  Ref. No. 24-78
 1.
Response to Council Motion – Coyle Creek Stormwater Management Facility (24-78).            Ref. No. 24-78  (Remove From Block)
 4.4
AGENCIES, BOARDS, COMMISSIONS AND COMMITTEE REPORTS - NIL
 5.
COMMITTEE-OF-THE-WHOLE (OPEN)/AGENDA BLOCK
 5.1
Request to Approve - Records Classification and Retention By-law and Schedule.   Ref. No. 23-50   (See By-law 7.1)
 5.2
Request for Funding – New Recreation and Community Complex Project Class C Cost Estimate.   Ref. No. 26-19
 5.3
Tender Award - 2026 Pavement Rejuvenation Program. Ref. No. 26-55
 5.4
Tender Award - Ontario Road Sanitary Trunk Sewer - Phase II. Ref. No. 26-62
 5.5
Update to Traffic and Parking By-law 89-2000 – Definitions, Designated Fire Routes and Private Property.   Ref. No. 26-22   (See By-law 7.2)
 6.
NEW BUSINESS 
 6.1
Dr. Raj Khanuja, Founder - National Dental Care Day re: requests to declare October 10, 2026 as ""National Dental Care Day"".   Ref. No. 26-6
 RECOMMENDATION:
THAT Council declares October 10, 2026, as ""National Dental Care Day"" in the City of Welland.
6.2
Michael Macpherson, President, CUPE Local 2276 re: request to declare June 23, 2026 as ""Developmental Service Worker Appreciation Day"".   Ref. No. 26-6
 RECOMMENDATION:
THAT Council declares June 23, 2026, as ""Developmental Service Worker Appreciation Day"" in the City of Welland.
7.
BY-LAWS 
 7.1
A By-law to establish a new records retentions schedule for the City of Welland, and to repeal By-law No. 9314 and all amendments thereto.   Ref. No. 23-50
 (See Item 5.1)
7.2
A By-law to amend By-law 89-2000, being a By-law to Regulate Traffic and Parking in the City of Welland (Definitions in Section 1.1 and Schedules I and VI).   Ref. No. 26-22
 (See item 5.5)
7.3
A By-law to delegate certain powers and duties under the Municipal Act, 2001, S.O. 2001, and other acts as contained in Schedule ""A"" appended hereto. Ref. No. 02-160
 (Presented to General Committee on June 30, 2026).
8.
NOTICES OF MOTION
 8.1
COUNCILLOR MATTERS DISCUSSED WITH STAFF FOR REPORTING PURPOSES - (For the purpose of informing the public on a matter that has been discussed with staff since the previous Council Meeting)
 8.2
NOTICES OF MOTION (PREVIOUSLY SUBMITTED FOR DISCUSSION)
 1.
(Councillor Setaram)
 That Council directs staff to develop and install consistent educational and safety signage at all stormwater management facilities across the city, in consultation with the Green Advisory Committee, to enhance public awareness of their function and applicable safety considerations, and that such signage be adopted as a standard requirement for all new stormwater management facilities constructed within the city.                       Ref. No. 24-78
2.
(Councillor Fokkens)
 THAT Council approves the addition of red flashers atop the stop signs at the intersection of Forkes Rd and Elm St – heading East and West.  Ref. No. 26-22
3.
(Councillor DiMarco)
 WHEREAS, the City of Welland ignored the affected residents questions and concerns and proceeded to build a pathway behind their homes who reside on Cedar Pk Dr, Whiteoak Cres, Madison Ct and Jackson Court; and
WHEREAS, there was no public consultation for this particular proposal for the affected residents until the project was received by council for information, they began the early stages while, the residents questions and concerns were once again ignored; and 
WHEREAS, it was not until approximately seven months after the early stages had begun was there a zoom meeting where residents were asked to submit questions prior to the meeting that was hosted by a third patty. The hosts, while scrolling through the questions submitted, answering random questions seemingly to their liking and the curfewed meeting ended; and
WHEREAS, affected residents were furious after the zoom meeting and demanded an in person meeting which the city did have and those who attended stated afterwards that the meeting did take place while stating that ""it was held just so that the city could state that an information meeting was held, and nothing was addressed afterwards"" and the preliminary work continued; and 
WHEREAS, and shortly after the clearing of the land began and after we were informed that 3 trees would be removed which ended in approx. 32 healthy trees were removed which totally contradicts matters when the city does have a ""tree canopy"" policy; and 
WHEREAS, fast forward to last early summer when the actual work began, the original plan was altered numerous times by the city, heavy equipment was used just feet away from the residents property, homes vibrated and a few residents sustained property damage; and 
WHEREAS, the trail proceeded despite its total contradiction of a report council had received CPTED (Crime Prevention thru Environmental Design) prior to the beginning of work and when trying to get an explanation on behalf of residents that were aware of such, it was explained that council did not approve it, rather it was received for information and work continued; and
WHEREAS, since its opening to the public the affected residents who for a number of years, some approx. 50 had taken care of this approx. 50' green space as ifrit was their own that allowed the city to concentrate on other green spaces and our parks and also when the city could not access due to the wet condition of the land and further concerns regarding the lack of cutting in comparison to how it was cared for by residents and with Ontario Health issuing a warning regarding the ticks and soon to be upon us mosquito residents have cautiously used their backyards.
FURTHERMORE, currently and seeing some are using this trail there has been a number of incidents and the affected residents have serious concerns for the safety of their young ones, garbage, dog feces, dogs although leashed confronting residents dogs who are in their own fenced yards, motorcycles using the trail one that narrowly missed there two young children, cyclists at a good rate of speed on more than one occasion at times forcing pedestrians off the trail, the potential of someone having to escape the trail can only be achieved thru private property which could lead to a confrontation, and the concern of their possessions on their property that has not been an issue until this trail was built, the lack of lighting, and with the grade of the trail that was first to have been crushed limestone that was changed to asphalt, water from the current amount of rain we've had and no doubt in the future the water does not flow to the north ditch, as it once did, rather it backs up and sits on residents prope1ty making it a problem as they try to maintain their lawn.
THEREFORE BE IT RESOLVED THAT COUNCIL on behalf of the affected residents that have been ignored by this unnecessary, unwanted and unsafe trail instruct staff to completely remove it, have the lands regrated to properly drain into the north ditch as before and not onto residents property, have trees replanted and the ground seeded to allow the residents to once again enjoy their backyards in a healthy and safe manner.  Ref. No. 99-99
4.
(Councillor Chiocchio)
 THAT staff explore any Provincial or Federal funding that can help the businesses at West Main Street and Ontario Road affected by the ongoing construction and report back to Council.   Ref. No.  26-58
5.
(Councillor Chiocchio)
 THAT staff provide a specific costing report as to the cost of fencing, dredging and landscaping specifically to Coyle Creek Stormwater Management Pond.  Ref. No. 24-78
6.
(Councillor McLeod)
 WHEREAS, ballot questions may be asked of the municipal electorate each election cycle; and   
WHEREAS, the Municipal Act governs the parameters of questions to ensure they are clearly understood; and further
WHEREAS, the Municipal Act governs  strict deadlines of which a council may request questions be added to the ballot. 
NOW THEREFORE BE IT RESOLVED, THAT THE COUNCIL OF THE CITY OF WELLAND be presented with a staff report a minimum of six months in advance of the deadline, so that the council can consider any question or questions it may wish to ask of the people it serves.  Ref. No. 26-43
8.3
CALL FOR NOTICES OF MOTION (TO BE INTRODUCED AT THE NEXT SCHEDULED COUNCIL MEETING)
 9.
CORPORATE REPORTS
 9.1
MAYOR'S REPORT
 9.2
COUNCILLORS WARD REPORT/ACTIVITIES - (2 minutes per Councillor)
 9.3
CHIEF ADMINISTRATIVE OFFICER'S REPORT
 10.
CONFIRMATORY BY-LAW
 10.1
A By-law to adopt, ratify and confirm proceedings of the Council of the Corporation of the City of Welland at its meeting held on the 14 day of July, 2026.
 11.
ADJOURNMENT
