and gates at the Jim Uffelmann Off-Leash 
Dog Park, and replacement of the 
RancheHouse boilers with a more efficient 
system. Snow-clearing and ice-control routes 
have been increased from three to four to 
improve consistency of coverage this winter. 
The report also noted that the Operations site 
and yard have reached capacity, which is 
increasing reliance on contracted services as 
the Town continues to grow. A dedicated 
update on Utilities will be provided to Council 
in May 2027. 
Delegation ~ Cochrane Library Board 
Chuck Love, Chair of the Cochrane Library 
Board, presented to Council on the impact of 
recent budget proposals on public access, 
programming and community services. The 
Library Board is requesting an 18% budget 
increase to address rising operating costs, 
maintain existing services and begin 
addressing essential facility needs. 
Council accepted the presentation for 
information. 
Cochrane Economic Development 10-Year 
Strategy (2026–2036) 
Council received the draft Cochrane 
Economic Development 10-Year Strategy for 
information. The Strategy sets the Town’s 
long-range framework for business growth 
and retention, economic diversification, 
investment attraction and long-term 
community prosperity, and is rooted in 
Envision Cochrane 2050. It was developed by 
Colliers Project Leaders in collaboration with 
Administration and informed by more than 
450 community touchpoints, including a 
community survey, focus groups with 
business and community leaders, and 
interviews with members of Council. 
The Strategy identifies five target sectors: 
tourism; creative economy; technology and 
innovation; light industrial and specialized 
manufacturing; and distribution, logistics and 
regional hub. It does not approve individual 
projects or commit Council to specific 
funding, which remain subject to future 
Council direction and annual budget 
approval. 
Administration will return to Council by June 
30, 2027 with a full performance 
measurement framework and will provide the 
first annual progress report by December 
2027. 

 
 
 
November 16, 2026 
Regular Council 
Bylaw 17/2026 ~ Rates, Charges & Fees 
Bylaw 17/2026 proposes to consolidate the 
Town’s municipal rates, fees and charges, 
which are currently spread across individual 
bylaws, administrative schedules and the 
budget, into a single bylaw. This includes fees 
for animal and business licensing, cemetery 
services, fire services, planning and 
development applications, safety codes 
permits, water and wastewater utilities, 
transit, waste management, facility bookings 
and recreation. The bylaw also amends 15 
existing bylaws to reference the consolidated 
fee schedule and sets consistent rules for 
payments, refunds, deposits and unpaid 
amounts. The bylaw is proposed to come into 
effect January 1, 2027. 
Council gave First Reading to Bylaw 17/2026.",📋 Agenda (* Minutes Pending),2026-11-23
COCHRANE,custom_html_pdf,https://www.cochrane.ca/sites/default/files/2026-11/Regular%20Council%20Meeting%20Highlights%20-%20November%209%2C%202026.pdf,"November 9, 2026 
Regular Council 
Greystone Area C Neighborhood Plan 
Amendment  
Council adopted amendments to the 
Greystone Development Area “C” 
Neighbourhood Plan that were proposed in 
order to reflect a Land Use Amendment 
application for Greystone Phase 9 (Bylaw 
15/2026) which is being brought forward to 
Council in conjunction with this application. 
The Land Use Amendment application 
proposed a redesignation of 8 Clarke Terrace 
from the General Industrial District (I-G) to the 
Business Industrial District (I-B). 
Bylaw 15/2026 ~ Greystone Phase 9 Land Use 
Amendment  
Bylaw 15/2026 proposed to re-designate the 
subject site from the General Industrial 
District (I-G) to the Business Industrial District 
(I-B) in the community of Greystone. This land 
use amendment is intended to match the 
land use district of the subject site with the 
land use district of the adjacent property to 
the south at 4 Clarke Terrace. It is the intent of 
the applicant to consolidate the two 
properties in order to proceed with a 
development permit application for one 
large, more cohesive industrial development 
with the Business Industrial District (I-B) 
designation. 
Council gave Second and Third Reading to 
Bylaw 15/2026. 
2027-2028 Civic Leadership & Engagement 
Pilot Framework  
Pending approval of the draft 2027 budget, 
Council approved Civic Leadership and 
Engagement Pilot Framework, including the 
Youth Council and Citizen Panel pilots, youth 
representation on Council committees, and 
approved the Citizen Panel Terms of 
Reference; and direct Administration to issue 
a Request for Interest for Leadership 
Cochrane in Q1 2027 and report the results to 
Council before entering into any delivery 
agreement.  
Non-Residential Development 
Planning Services investigated and provided 
options for ways to address barriers that may 
restrict the advancement of planning 
applications for the purpose of non-
residential development. The focus was about 
finding manageable, cost-effective solutions 
to shorten and promote non-residential 
development in Cochrane. Planning Services 
engaged other Town departments to 
understand process challenges, outdated 
policies and procedures and collaborated on 
efficiencies to identify and address gaps in 
the current processes and policies. 
Administration will return to Council in July 
2027 to provide an update on process 
improvements. 
 

 
 
 
November 9, 2026 
Regular Council 
Off-Site Levy Optimization & Infrastructure 
Timing 
Administration responded to a notice of 
motion directing a review of Cochrane’s Off-
Site Levy framework. Opportunities to improve 
and accelerate the planning, inclusion and 
cost recovery of growth-related infrastructure 
while considering the legislative 
requirements, constraints, and financial 
implications of the levy program were 
recommended.  
Ongoing work is intended to support the 
inclusion or refinement of Cochrane’s library, 
fire hall, recreation, water treatment, 
transportation and stormwater infrastructure 
in the Off-Site Levy Bylaw. Proactive master 
planning, timely Council direction, 
stakeholder consultation and dedicated 
long-range planning resources provide the 
greatest opportunities to identify 
infrastructure earlier and accelerate cost 
recovery. Administration is targeting Off-Site 
Levy Bylaw updates every two years, with the 
next update anticipated in 2028. 
Financing Affordable Growth 
On October 9, 2026, the Mayor and 
Administration presented to Minister Dan 
Williams, Alberta’s Minister of Municipal 
Affairs, on the challenges fast-growing 
communities face in funding and financing 
growth-related infrastructure. 
This advocacy ties in with Council’s 
Community Infrastructure Advocacy Priority, 
specifically proportional funding and cost-
sharing tools for high-growth municipalities. 
Council added this as an advocacy priority 
for 2026–2030 because it was recognized 
that fast-growing communities like Cochrane 
often have to build infrastructure and deliver 
services before the tax base catches up to 
pay for them. Cochrane is advocating for 
funding and cost-sharing tools that keep 
pace with growth, so the cost of building a 
community isn’t carried by local property 
taxpayers alone.",📋 Agenda (* Minutes Pending),2026-11-23
