DAWSON,custom_html_pdf,https://www.dawsontownship.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Dawson-council-minutes-November-4th-2026.pdf,"The minutes of the regular meeting of Dawson Township Council held on Wednesday 
November 4th, 2026 at 7 pm in the Municipal Office.  
PRESENT Mayor D Hartnell. Councillors: B Langner, C Larocque & B Sands. Clerk-
Treasurer: P W Giles. Road Supt R Asselin. Visitors: C Krahn & R Sinninghe 
 
MINUTES #26-80 SANDS & LAROCQUE & CARRIED that the minutes of the Court of 
Revision of the Rempel Municipal Drainage Works Improvement 2026 and the 
regular meeting of Council held on October 7th, 2026, be approved as printed.  
ACCOUNTS #26-81 LANGNER & KEMPF & CARRIED that the accounts in the amount of 
$127,117.11 and representing the disbursements for the month of October be 
approved for payment.  
BY-LAW 502 #26-82 LANGNER & KEMPF & CARRIED that By-law No 502, being the 
Spohn Municipal Drain Extension, 2026 Cost Amending By-law, be enacted. 
 
BY-LAW 503 #26-83 LANGER & SANDS & CARRIED that By-law No 503, being the Branch 
5 Assessment Amending By-law, be enacted. 
 
BY-LAW 504 #26-84 SANDS & LAROCQUE & CARRIED that By-law 504, being the Drain 
C Engebretsen Drain Abandonment By-law, be enacted. 
 
OTHER 
The Rd Supt. reported on the repairs to the plow truck, drain brushing, beaver dam 
removal, installation of winter plow equipment, and quote received of crushing 
gravel in the Blue Pit. Council will consider for next year. Winter sand is being 
stock piled. The Clerk-Treasurer reported on: the next fire board meeting, the 
balance of taxes outstanding, municipal election results, landfill study is expected 
by the end of November, receipt of OMPF funding of $116,400, NORDS grant of 
$161036.26, the filing of library operating & pay equity report, 2027 OPP 
increasing from $101,128 to $112,252, and work is being done on the Community 
Risk Assessment. Council is awaiting contractor price for wayside park 
improvements for 2027. An enquiry is to be made to the recycling contractor to 
clarify if recycling bins can be provided to residents to help collect recyclables. 
Upcoming emergency measures training and exercise to be held on December 10th 
in Rainy River. The ratepayers on Drain C of the Engebretsen Drain had requested 
to abandon the drain, See By-law 503. Mayor Hartnell reported on upcoming 
NOMA meeting and recent RRDMA executive meeting. Councillor Langner gave 
an update on DRRSB matters. Mayor Hartnell thanked the existing Councillors on 
their dedicated community service and wished them the best in the future. 
 
ADJOURN #26-85 LANGNER & KEMPF & CARRIED that the meeting be adjourned at 8:03 
p.m. 
  
 _____________________________ Mayor ________________________Clerk",📄 Official Minutes,2026-11-23
DAWSON,custom_html_pdf,https://www.dawsontownship.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Dawson-council-minutes-October-7-2026.pdf,"The minutes of the Committee of Adjustment, and regular meeting of Dawson 
Township Council held on Wednesday October 7th, 2026 at 7 pm in the Municipal 
Office.  
PRESENT Mayor D Hartnell. Councillors: B Langner, C Larocque & B Sands. Clerk-
Treasurer: P W Giles. Road Supt R Asselin. Visitors: D&D Tank. 
 
CONSENT The Clerk-Treasurer gave an overview of the proposed consent by D&D Tank file 
34B2602. The application was circulated on the website and Facebook. No inquiries 
have been received from the public regarding the application. The proponents spoke 
in favour of the application and clarified questions of the committee.  
34B2602 
#26-71 LANGNER & SANDS & CARRIED that Council grants provisional 
approval to consent application 34B2602 D&D Tank, subject to the attached 5 
conditions. Meeting was declared closed at 7:10 p.m. 
MINUTES #26-72 LAROCQUE & LANGNER & CARRIED that the minutes of the regular 
meeting of Council held on September 2nd 2026, be approved as printed.  
ACCOUNTS #26-73 LANGNER & SANDS & CARRIED that the accounts in the amount of 
$144,437.44 be approved for payment.  
BY-LAW 500 #26-74 LANGNER & LAROCQUE & CARRIED that By-law No 500, being the 
Rempel Municipal Drainage Works Improvement 2026 By-law, be given third and 
final reading and be enacted. 
 
BY_LAW 501 #26-75 SANDS & LAROCQUE & CARRIED that By-law No 501, being the 
NORDS 2026 By-law, be enacted. 
 
TIME ZONE #26-76 LANGNER & SANDS & CARRIED that Council supports the Township 
remaining on Central Time, aligned with Manitoba, and requests that the Province 
of Ontario take the necessary steps to permit this arrangement to continue. 
 
FIRE 
#26-77 LAROCQUE & LANGNER & CARRIED that Council supports the Town 
of Plympton-Wyoming, requesting the Province of Ontario to establish sustainable 
provincial grant funding for fire services in Ontario.  
 
DRAINS 
#26-78 SANDS & LANGNER & CARRIED that Council receives and approves the 
report of engineer Jacob Rooke from R J Burnside, pursuant to Section 65(10) of the 
Drainage Act for the reapportionment of the assessment schedule for the 
Government Road Municipal Drainage Works Branch 5 and the Government Road 
Extension Drainage Works.  
 
 
OTHER 
The Rd Supt. reported on the condition of the graders. We are still awaiting the 
return of the plow truck. He is to check into prices to crush our own gravel in Blue 
Pit. Mike completed the Due Diligence Course and will work on other courses. 
They have set up the pad for hosting the recycle depot. The Clerk-Treasurer 

reported on: the next fire board meeting, the balance of taxes outstanding, no 
appeals on the Rempel Drain, awaiting the grant application for the Spohn Drain 
before we can bill out the costs, the garage repairs have been completed, the 
repairs to the Wayside rest stop will begin once the contractor returns form 
vacation, awaiting further update to mapping and the report from the consultant in 
order the proceed to adopt the Official Plan and Zoning By-Law, awaiting the 
landfill report, the garage said they would try to get the plow truck into the shop 
next week, the engineer’s report to adjust the sharing ratio for maintenance costs 
for future maintenance on the Branch 5 Government Road Drain and Government 
Road Drain Extension was received and circulated to property owners, in order to 
borrow trust funds to use towards a Columbarium, we would need to prove we 
cannot secure funds elsewhere, awaiting MTO reports on the request to lower the 
speed limit in Pinewood, and the request to approve placement of a stainless steel 
monument in the cemetery. Mayor Hartnell reported on a request to reestablish the 
North Fire Hall. However, due to anticipated costs for equipment, vehicles, and 
building repairs, it would not be feasible at this time to proceed with such a 
request. The Mayor also reported on recent NOMA meeting and upcoming 
RRDMA executive meeting. Councillor Langner gave an update on DRRSB 
matters. 
 
ADJOURN #26-79 LANGNER & SANDS & CARRIED that the meeting be adjourned at 8:53 
p.m. 
  
 _____________________________ Mayor ________________________Clerk",📄 Official Minutes,2026-11-23
DAWSON,custom_html_pdf,https://www.dawsontownship.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Dawson-council-minutes-September-2-2026.pdf,"A meeting to consider the engineer’s report for the Rempel Municipal Drainage 
Works Improvement 2026 and the minutes of the regular meeting of Dawson 
Township Council held on Wednesday September 2, 2026 at 7 pm in the Municipal 
Office.  
PRESENT Mayor D Hartnell. Councillors: B Langner, C Larocque & B Sands. Clerk-
Treasurer: P W Giles. Road Supt absent. Visitors Jurgen Schmutz and Jacob Rooke 
attended by telephone 
 
REPORT 
Mayor Hartnell opened the meeting and explained the purpose of the meeting and 
then invited comments or questions. J Schmutz has a question on the appropriate 
allocation of costs for the solar farm and his farmland. Jacob will provide his notes 
on this matter for Jurgen and the other solar farms within the drainage area. The 
meeting to consider the report was closed at 7:10pm. 
 
MINUTES #26-62 LANGNER & SANDS & CARRIED that the minutes of the regular meeting 
of Council held on August 5th be approved as printed.  
ACCOUNTS #26-63 SANDS & LAROCQUE & CARRIED that the accounts in the amount of 
$143680.82 be approved for payment.  
BY-LAW 497 #26-64 SANDS & LAROCQUE & CARRIED that By-law No 497, being the 
Spohn Municipal Drain Extension 2026 By-law, be given third reading and be 
enacted. 
BY-LAW 500 #26-65 LANGNER & LAROCQUE & CARRIED that By-law No 500, being the 
Rempel Municipal Drainage Works Improvement 2026 By-law, be given first and 
second reading and be provisionally adopted. 
 
COURT 
#26-66 SANDS & LANGNER & CARRIED that pursuant to Section 97 of the 
Drainage Act the following be appointed to the Court of Revision for the Rempel 
Municipal Drainage Works Improvement 2026 project; Cory Larocque, Bill 
Langner, and Telford Advent from the Township of Morley. 
 
DONATION #26-67 LANGNER & LAROCQUE & CARRIED that Council authorizes a 
donation of $100.00 to support the Rainy River Recreation Mini Fishing Derby Sept 
19th 2026. 
 
OTHER 
The Rd Supt was absent. The Clerk-Treasurer reported on the next fire board 
meeting, the balance of taxes outstanding. Municipal election advanced voting for 
October 17th and October 26, 2026. There are two candidates for Mayor and 5 
candidates for Councillor. Successful enrolment in MTO program for financing 
responses for accidents on provincial highways. 2027 OPP increase will be limited 
to 11%. An inquiry was received and responded to for the Sider tax sale. A request 
was received to allow stainless steel monuments within our cemetery. A picture of 
the proposed monument will be requested from the supplier. The non-compliance 
for building permit application was successfully resolved with the issuance of two 

permits. Finally, the plow truck harness has been repaired at a dealer in Virginia, 
MN and is awaiting reinstallation in the truck. Funding request for Just for Kicks 
Trip to Florida was denied. While a $100 donation was approved for the kids mini 
fishing derby in Rainy River. The mayor reported on the recent Community 
Gathering at Assabaska Heritage Park. The attendance was less than expected and 
the funding request will be forwarded to RRFDC. Council will consider the use of 
drones for brush spraying on drains and along roads. Council will proceed with an 
offer to purchase the rented office space from the present Clerk-Treasurer. Council 
was presented with alternate propane pricing and will consider the matter. Council 
will request a meeting with the Drainage Superintendent in the new year to review 
the five-year drain maintenance program. Councillor Larocque requested Council 
to consider replacement/repair of the Municipal Cemetery and other signs. Council 
wishes to have the Road Superintendent Assistant to enroll in Supervisor courses 
in preparation for future appointment as Road Superintendent. Council wants the 
stumps and debris cleared at the wayside park in Sleeman. Council is interested in 
procurement and funding of a columbarium for the Blue Cemetery. AMCTO send 
a letter acknowledging 45 year membership in the association by Patrick Giles.  
 
 
ADJOURN #26-68 SANDS & LANGNER & CARRIED that the meeting be adjourned at 8:45 
p.m. 
  
 _____________________________ Mayor ________________________Clerk",📄 Official Minutes,2026-11-23
DAWSON,custom_html_pdf,https://www.dawsontownship.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Dawson-council-minutes-August-5th-2026.pdf,"A Court of Revision for the Spohn Municipal Drain Extension 2026 and the minutes 
of the regular meeting of Dawson Township Council held on Wednesday August 5th 
2026 at 7 pm in the Municipal Office.  
PRESENT Mayor D Hartnell. Councillors:  L Kempf, B Langner, C Larocque & B Sands. 
Clerk-Treasurer: P W Giles. Road Supt absent. No one attend the Court of Revision 
starting at 7pm however Mr. A Rummel attended the office prior to the sitting to 
seek clarification on the purpose of the Court and clarification on his assessment(s) 
under the report. 
 
COURT  
#26-53 LAROCQUE & KEMPF & CARRIED that pursuant to Section 97 of the 
Drainage Act the following be appointed to the Court of Revision for the Spohn 
Municipal Drain Extension 2026 project: Bill Langner, Brent Sands and Lewis 
Kempf.  
CLOSED 
#26-54 SANDS & LANGNER & CARRIED that there being no written appeals and 
no one present making an appeal to the Court of Revision for the Spohn Municipal 
Drain Extension 2026 report, the court be closed at 7:05 p.m.  
MINUTES #26-55 LANGNER & KEMPF & CARRIED that the minutes of the regular meeting 
of Council held on July 8th 2026 including the minutes for the meeting to consider 
the Rempel Drainage Works and the Spohn Municipal Drain Extension 2026 
reports, be approved as printed.  
ACCOUNTS #26-56 SANDS & LAROCQUE & CARRIED that the accounts in the amount of 
$271,370.24 and representing disbursements for the month of July 2026 and the 
amended June disbursements in the amount of $208,171.65, be approved for 
payment.  
LAWYER #26-57 LAROCQUE & LANGNER & CARRIED that Council authorizes the hiring 
of a lawyer to represent the Township in the building code issues case and the 
charges that have been laid by the Chief Building Official.  
STORMONT #26-58 LANGNER & SANDS & CARRIED that Council endorses the resolution 
of the Township of Stormont requesting the Province of Ontario to permanently 
double the Ontario Community Infrastructure Funding.  
 
LAME DUCK #26-59 KEMPF & LAROCQUE & CARRIED Whereas Section 275 of the 
Municipal Act, 2001 restricts certain expenditures and liabilities during a Lame 
Duck period; and Whereas Council wishes to ensure the continued and orderly 
operation of the Township during such a period; Therefore Be It Resolved That the 
Clerk-Treasurer is authorized to approve expenditures and incur liabilities on behalf 
of the Township up to $25,000 per transaction, provided such expenditures are 
within the approved municipal budget and are otherwise permitted by law; and 
further that any expenditures exceeding $25,000 unless otherwise authorize under 
the Municipal Act, 2001 shall be require Council approval.  
 

LOCUM 
#26-60 KEMPF & LAROCQUE & CARRIED that pursuant to the recommendation 
of the Locum House Committee, Council agrees to the proposal to have out joint 
owned 2 vacant lots appraised and offered for sale.  
 
OTHER 
The Rd Supt was on vacation. The Clerk-Treasurer reported on the next fire board 
meeting, the balance of taxes outstanding, OMPF payment of $116,400, a request 
from a property owner in Sleeman seeking clarification on whether Council would 
entertain a rezoning application of their property, MTO still awaiting data on 
possible speed limit reduction through the Village of Pinewood, Council is willing. 
Councillor Larocque requesting that Council budget for replacement of municipal 
signage including our cemeteries. Council is seeking information on installation of 
a columbarium in the Blue Cemetery. Council discussed the funding from RRFDC 
to host a community gathering at Assabaska Heritage Park for Aug 29th and the 
various aspects to consider with the invitations, food items and preparations, prizes 
etc. Staff will send out invitations and seek firm numbers of people attending the 
event. Councillor Kempf reported on a recent locum house meeting. R Sawchuk 
requested spraying of the Sleeman Drain in order to control brush and future costs. 
Council discussed the sheep running at large in the area of Blue Rd #2. Council 
discussed the upcoming court case on the charges related to a ratepayer and non- 
compliance with building permit requirements and recognized the need for a 
lawyer to represent the municipality interests in the matter.  
 
ADJOURN #26-61 SANDS & LANGNER & CARRIED that the meeting be adjourned at 8:50 
p.m. 
  
 _____________________________ Mayor ________________________Clerk",📋 Council Document,2026-11-23
