Land Acknowledgement
Open Session Report
Background:
A land acknowledgement can serve an educational purpose. To most, the
lndigenous history of the area or the treaty relationships that exist today is
unknown. By adopting a land acknowledgement, and placing it in our Municipal
Buildings, the municipality helps promote awareness of the local history and
encouriges residents to learn more about the lndigenous Nations connected to
the region.
Gomments:
With adoption of the policy and acknowledgement, the following would be
proposed, in summary:
1) The Acknowledgement be posted in the municipal office, fire hall, Dawn
Euphemia Community Centre, and the Shetland Park - the library has
been eliminated as the County currently has their acknowledgement in the
library.
2) The Acknowledgement be posted on the municipal website.
Recommendation:
That the following policy be approved as presented.

Policy Title:
Land Acknowledgement
Approval Date:
Effective Date:
Last Revised
Date:
2026-11-16
2026-11-16
2026-11-16
1. Policy Statement
To Acknowledge the history of the First Nations
2, Purpose / Background Information
This document sets out the Corporation of the Township of Dawn Euphemia's policy for the
acknowledgement of the contributions and history of the First Nations on whose historical
and traditional lands Dawn-Euphemia now situate.
3. Application
4. Definitions
ln This Policy, the following terms have the following meaning:
(a) ,,Council"" means the body of elected Council members whom together form the
elected council of the Municipality.
(b) ,'First Nations"" means the first inhabitants of Canada and, locally, means the
Aamjiwnaang First Nation, Walpole lsland First Nation (Bkejwanong Territory) and
Ketl-e and Slony Point First Nation (Chippewas (Anishnaabeg) of Kettle and Stony
Point).
(c) ,,First Nation Peoples"" means the First Nations' members and their ancestors
including, without limitation, their Chippewa, Ojibwe, Potawatomi, and Odawa
antecedents.
5. Policy Details
5.1 Policv
The Corporation of the Township of Dawn Euphemia acknowledges, with respect, the
history, culture, and spirituality of the First Nations and First Nations Peoples on
whose traditional lands the Municipality is now situated, with whom all share
harmoniously, in peace and in friendship, and with whom we have an obligation to
protect the environment for this and future generations.
Page 1 of 4

5.2 Traditional Lands Acknowledgement
a) ln furtherance of the statement set out above, the Corporation of the Township of
Dawn Euphemia hereby adopts the traditional lands acknowledgement attached
hereto as Appendix ""A"" (hereinafter, the ""Acknowledgement"").
6. Use/Posting of Acknowledqement
a) lt is hereby authorized that the Acknowledgement be included and posted as follows
Posted on the Municipality's public website, www.dawneuphemia.ca
a,
b. lncluded in the Municipality's Strategic Plan, including any amended versions of
the plan as updated from time to time, and other relevant strategic planning
documents associated with Municipal services;
c. Posted publicly, at a conspicuous location and the entrance of the following
Municipal buildings where the Acknowledgement is likely to come to the attention
of members of the public:
- MunicipalAdministrationBuilding
- Dawn Euphemia Fire Hall
- Dawn Euphemia Community Centre
7. lnterpretation
(a)This Policy shall be interpreted and enforced in a manner consistent with the
objective of the Policy set out in the subject- heading Policy Statement above
and, specifically, in a manner that advocates for a respectful, constructive
acknowledgement of the history of First Nations and First Nations Peoples on the
lands within Lambton County and Dawn Euphemia.
8. Scooe of Policv
(a)This document sets out Council's policy on the subject-matter hereof. This Policy
is not intended and does not serve to amend any other policy. ln the event of any
inconsistency between this Policy and any other policy of the Township, the policy
that directly address the subject matter under consideration shall govern.
