John Collison O.R.O., Public Works Superintendent
New Waterline Construction Dawn Mills Road (South of Kent Line)
OPEN SESSION REPORT
Background:
The p-urpose of this report is to advise Council of a request to extend the Township of Dawn-
Euphemia's watermain approximately B50M south of Kent Line along Dawn Mills Road, across
the municipal boundary into Chatham-Kent, to service two residential properties.
Comments:
A Chatham-Kent landowner near the Dawn-Euphemia boundary has requested municipal water
service due to a failing well. Several attempts to establish alternative wells have been
unsuccessful in locating a reliable water source. A second neighbouring property owner has
also expressed interest in connecting to the municipal water System.
The proposed 850-metre extension would consist of a 50 mm (2-inch) watermain. Preliminary
hydraulit modelling by Dobbin Engineering indicates minimal impacts on existing system
pressures.
Estimated Costs
The esttrnated installation cost is $90/M (r10%), resulting in an estimated construction costof
$76,500 to $84,150, excluding additional expenses and applicable taxes.
Additional costs include:
Engineering and preparation of the required Form 1'
Two valves for isolation and blow-offs/maintenance.
Residential water meters.
Temporary heating and cold-weather protection, if required
Watermain disinfection and super-chlorination'
Pressure testing and bacteriological sampling before commissioning
All project costs are intended to be
approved cost-sharing arrangement'
shared by the two benefiting property owners through an
Water Supplv and Boundarv Considerations
itionfromitsupstreamwatersupply.Councilmustconsider
whether to allocate capacity to properties outside Dawn-Euphemia or retain it for future
residential and development-needs within the Township. An acceptable boundary agreement
with Chatham-Kent, consistent with existing agreements, would be required before proceeding.
Hvdraulic Modellinq from Dobbin Engineerinq
sbmmwatermainservingfourhomes,basedon400
L/personTday, three persons per household, and a peaking factor of four.
Average daily demand: 0.056 L/s
Peak-hour demand: 0.222 Lls
Nov 2 2026
Dawn Mills Waterline Extension - 2026-27

Pressure impact:
Lambton Line and Dawn Mills Road: 33.3 to 32.8 psi (0.5 psi decrease).
Kent Line and Dawn Mills Road: 43.2to 41.6 psi (1.6 psi decrease).
Watermain velocity: 0.11 m/s at peak-hour demand, considered acceptable.
Gonsultation:
Karl Robinson, Robinson Farm Drainage was consulted
Ray Dobbin and Jacob Tetreault, Dobbin Engineering
Financial lmplications:
There are no financial implications, as all inputs are paid by the users.
Strategic Planning lmplications:
There are no direct strategic planning implications. However, Council should consider the long-
term implications of allocating the Township's available water supply to properties outside its
municipal boundaries, particularly in relation to future growth and servicing requirements within
Dawn-Euphemia.
Recommendation: That Council consider the following options:
Option 1 - Approve the Watermain Extension
That Council support, in principle, the extension of the Township of Dawn-Euphemia's
watermain approximately B50M south of Kent Line on Dawn Mills Road into the Municipality of
Chatham-Kent to provide municipal water service to two residential properties, subject to the
following conditions:
. All project costs are paid by the benefiting property owners.
. An acceptable boundary agreement is established between the Township of Dawn-
Euphemia and the Municipality of Chatham-Kent.
. All required engineering, regulatory, and municipal approvals are obtained.
. The available water supply allocation and impacts on future servicing needs within
Dawn-Euphemia are reviewed and deemed acceptable.
And that the Public Works Superintendent be authorized to proceed with the necessary
engineering review, cost estimates, boundary agreement discussions, and other preliminary
steps required to advance the project.
Option 2 - Retain Water Supply Capacity for Township Needs
That Council declines the request to extend the Township's watermain into the Municipality of
Chatham-Kent and retain the available water supply capacity and infrastructure for future
residential and development needs within Dawn-Euphemia Township.
