Computer Software System Gonversion - Munisoft - January
2027 - GO LIVE
OPEN SESSION REPORT
Background:
The p-urpose of this report is to provide Council with a summary of new Computer Software
system for the MuniciPalitY.
October 7th Deputy Treasurer and I participated in MFOA webinar on computer software
resources. Different municipalities across Ontario were participating in this webinar. We
heard from the majority of municipalities that are leaving or going to leave Central Square
who our current software is from. Poor support and just not happy with how the programs
run and the ease of operation. Most of the municipalities are leaning to convert to Munisoft.
Munisoft is a Canadian software company that has been around for 4l years and is built
for municipalities. lt is fairly easy to navigate around. They have great support and all new
employees onboarding wi[fr a municipality are forced to do web based training and one on
one training with a trainer when they are hired'
Jami Hornbostel-Slade and I are familiar with Munisoft as we used this previously in Oil
Springs. (it is designed for smaller municipalities) lt has allthe features that we would need
nere to perform ow financial duties. They provide great service and training to employees.
I have d'iscussed the software problem with our Auditing Firm. They agree that the current
system is not the best.
I have spoken to TownSuite, another software supplier, and I have a called and discussed
with a municipality that is currently using this software to see what or if there are any issues
with the software. They explained TownSuite is just branching out to Ontario municipalities.
Unfortunately, TownSuite ls not able to offer their product to us as quickly as Munisoft and
the conversion is at a higher cost. $135,000 to onboard and roughly $23,000.O0/year cost
for licensing.
When we do the conversion, I want to completely redo the general ledger account numbers
and create monthly reports for council that are computer generated and easily readable'
Also, on October 7th, we had a 3 hour demonstration of Munisoft with all office staff.
The trainer took us through most of the modules and demonstrated how to set up accounts,
post payments, run tax bills etc.

At the end of the demonstration staff were unanimous and asked the trainer when we could
go live with this software. We were told they have a January 2027 availability, or the next
one would be fall of 2027.
I propose we work together and make the switch for January 2027 '
I also proposing that in January we switch to Easypay payroll system - we would be
responsible for all aspects of the payroll system including T-4 preparation. Currently our
2026 payroll system fees are estimated to be $2750.00 plus our time to process.
Easypay is approximately $365.00/year and is user friendly to navigate and we would be
responsible for all payrollfunctions and T-4's.
That Council agrees to the implementation of a new software system for January 2027,for
Munisoft software supplier with the following prices and that the Administrator-Clerk-
Treasurer sign contracts to proceed with implementation of Munisoft Software with a live
date of January 2027.:
Contract # 1 - Conversion of data from Asyst - $22,500 plus HST
Contract # 2 - Munisoft Software System - with 5 users - $47,568.00 plus HST
Yearly license fees - approximately $7914.00 starting in 2028

