Re: Raising Awareness of the Radon Gas in the Township of Dubreuilville 
Dear Mayor and CAO, 
Following the Board of Health’s resolution on preventing and responding to radon exposures in Algoma, 
I am writing to invite the Township of Dubreuilville to consider raising awareness on radon gas. 
What is Radon? 
Radon is a radioactive gas that can be present in homes across Canada. It forms naturally when uranium 
in soil and rock breaks down. Radon can enter homes and accumulate over time. Because radon cannot 
be seen, smelled, or tasted, testing is the only way to determine whether indoor levels are elevated. 
Approximately 1 in 6 homes in the central region of Canada have average radon levels at or above the 
Canadian radon guideline of 200 Bq/m³. Long-term exposure to radon gas is a significant health concern 
and is associated with approximately 3,000 lung cancer deaths each year in Canada. 
Support APH can provide for your Municipality 
APH has reviewed municipal websites across Algoma and did not identify radon-related information on 
your municipal website. To increase awareness and testing, APH can suggest radon information to 
include on your website and resources and opportunities for subsidy programs for radon mitigation in 
homes with elevated radon levels. APH is also available to consult on radon policy information for 
inclusion in official plans and related municipal documents. In the interim, residents of your township 
may be able to get a library card for free or at a fee to access radon monitors from public libraries in Sault 
Ste. Marie, Wawa, Elliot Lake and Blind River, depending on whether your municipality is in contact 
with these public libraries. This is particularly important during the Fall/Winter months, when Canadian 
homes are sealed against the weather and radon gas can potentially accumulate to high levels.   
Please contact Algoma Public Health’s Environmental Health Department 
at ehclerical@algomapublichealth.com for more information. Thank you for your time and consideration. 
We look forward to the opportunity to collaborate with you on this important public health initiative.  
For heathier communities, 

Radon Gas  
Township of Dubreuilville 
