Unfinished Business: 
CAO/Clerk Gorgerat advised that Council had been provided with an email chain between 
Mr. Gardner and a representative from the Invasive Species Centre confirming that the 
Township had secured the rental of the CD3 Watercraft Cleaning Machine for 2027. The 
Invasive Species Centre will follow-up with the Township regarding pricing at a later date. 
CAO/Clerk Gorgerat also advised that, on October 13th, the Round Lake Property Owners 
Association (RLPOA) invited Council and the public to attend the 2026 Round Lake: Lake 
Trout Stocking Initiative at 815 Red Rock Road from 12:30 p.m. to 1:00 p.m. and the event 
will included the official release of 11,500 lake trout yearlings to help enhance the local 
ecosystem. 

CAO/Clerk Gorgerat indicated that Public Works Superintendent Holly is seeking direction 
from Council regarding the placement of the painted 50 km signage on Mill Street; including 
the number of signs required and whether signage should be painted in both directions-
entering and exiting the town-or only in one direction.  Council directed that one be painted 
between McCarthy’s and Civic Street and the other by the Hydro Station coming from Hwy 
60 on to Mill Street. 
New Business: 
CAO/Clerk Gorgerat advised that Integrity Commissioner/Closed Meeting Investigator Tony 
Fleming had provided written notice of his retirement, effective December 31st. She sought 
Council’s direction on whether to continue with Cunningham Swan, issue a request for 
proposals (RFP) for a new Integrity Commissioner/Closed Meeting Investigator, or 
participate in a joint RFP with another municipality, as the Town of Renfrew had inquired 
whether the Township would be interested in participating in a joint process.  Council 
discussed the matter and directed that it be brought forward for consideration at the next 
Regular Council meeting. 
CAO/Clerk Gorgerat reported that it was brought to the Township’s attention that Ward 3 
English Public ballots were missing the question regarding the abolition of the ward system, 
the English Public school support designation beneath the ward number, instructions at the 
bottom of the ballot and other relevant information. The third-party vendor was notified 
immediately and is working to reprint the ballots and issue replacement vote-by-mail kits.  
CAO/Clerk Gorgerat advised that the replacement kits will include an insert printed on 
coloured paper explaining the error and providing instructions regarding the replacement 
kit, the front of the envelope will also include the statement, “Ward 03 English Public 
Replacement Vote by Mail Kit Enclosed for the 2026 Municipal Election in KHR” and public 
notice will be provided through the Township’s social media platforms, website, and two 
local newspapers.  CAO/Clerk Gorgerat further reported that the Township Solicitor had 
been advised of the situation and was supportive of the proposed approach and she had 
also contacted the Township’s advisor at the Ministry of Municipal Affairs and Housing 
(MMAH) to inquire whether the Ministry has an established protocol or recommended 
procedure for addressing ballot printing errors, including the omission of a ballot question, 
and to obtain any other relevant guidance. 
Council and Candidates Mr. Gravefell and McFalls discussed this matter at length where 
concerns were raised and discussed with CAO/Clerk Gorgerat. 
