Report Council – REC-2026-09.1 
 
Date:  
November 18, 2026  
 
 
 
   
    ☐  IN CAMERA 
 
Subject:  
Community Sport and Recreation Infrastructure Fund (CSRIF) Grant 
Submission 
 
Author:  
Jeff Johnston, Manager of Parks, Recreation & Facilities☒   OPEN SESSION 
 
 
RECOMMENDATION: 
BE IT RESOLVED THAT THE COUNCIL OF THE TOWN OF GANANOQUE SUPPORTS  
THE SUBMISSION OF A GRANT APPLICATION TO THE COMMUNITY SPORT AND 
RECREATION INFRASTRUCTURE FUND (CSRIF) FOR OPTION _____. 
 
AND FURTHER THAT THE AMOUNT OF $______________, TOWN SHARE, BE DRAWN 
FROM THE TOWN CAPITAL RESERVE, AS PRESENTED IN REPORT COUNCIL REC-
2026-09.1. 
 
STRATEGIC PLAN COMMENTS: 
Priority #1 – Provide Quality Core Services and Infrastructure – Strategy #2 – Prioritize the 
Implementation of the 10-year Asset Management Plan. 
BACKGROUND: 
On October 7, 2026, Staff presented Report Council-REC-2026-09 for Council’s 
consideration.  Recognizing that the current Council was in “Lame-Duck”, Motion #26-177 
was passed which directed staff to bring the Report back to the new Council on November 
18, 2026.  As directed, the Report is being presented to the new Council for consideration.  
 
The Community Sport and Recreation Infrastructure Fund (CSRIF) is a capital funding 
program delivered by the Ministry of Sport to revitalize existing community sport and 
recreation infrastructure and support the construction of new facilities across Ontario. 
 
The CSRIF first intake began in 2024 through two streams: 
▪ Stream 1:  Repair and Rehabilitation 
▪ Stream 2:  New Builds/Signature New Builds 
 
On August 17, 2026 the provincial government announced the opening of the second intake 
with a closing date for submissions on December 22, 2026. 
 
The purpose of Stream 1 is to extend the lifespan of existing community sport and 
recreation facilities/spaces and improve local programming and accessibility features to 
meet community need. 
 

Page 2 of 3 
 
As noted in the CSRIF guidelines, eligible projects for Stream 1 must: 
• 
Be for the repair and rehabilitation of a sport or recreation facility/space or other 
community facility/space in Ontario that offers sport or recreation programming: 
• 
Be for a community facility/space in Ontario that is or will be open primarily for use by 
the public for sport or recreation and will continue to be following completion of the 
project.  
 
Eligible projects may include projects that: 
• 
Extend the lifespan of existing community sport and recreation facilities and spaces: 
• 
Maximize the use of existing facilities (e.g., use of space, increasing hours of operation, 
enhancing functionality and/or participation rates); or 
• 
Improve health and safety, accessibility and environmental standards of existing 
facilities. 
 
The Town applied for the CSRIF grant in 2024 for the replacement of the elevator lift at the 
Lou Jeffries Community Centre but were unsuccessful in their bid. 
 
INFORMATION/DISCUSSION: 
Upon further review of replacing the accessible lift at the Lou Jeffries arena, a lift would not 
meet AODA standards and therefore a full renovation of the current setup and construction 
of an elevator would meet the AODA criteria for an estimated cost of one million dollars. 
 
Therefore, Town staff have reviewed the program guidelines and the Town’s recreation 
infrastructure and they have determined the following options for consideration instead of 
the lift replacement: 
 
Option #1:  The revitalization of the BMX Track to a Pump Track.  As noted in Council 
Report REC-2026-08, installations of Pump Tracks are becoming increasingly 
popular in Canada as it provides access for all ages, abilities and 
demographics to ride on their bikes, scooters, and skateboards in a safe 
space and off the streets, while challenging and enhancing their skills.  The 
conversion of the defunct BMX track to a pump track checks all the eligibility 
boxes for this grant. 
 
Option #2:  Expansion of the outdoor sports courts to add additional pickleball courts.  The 
Tennis and Pickleball groups continue to grow in participation levels and they 
are outgrowing the current court configuration.  An addition of 4 to 6 tennis 
and pickleball courts would aid in the congestion of the courts often seen 
throughout the spring, summer and early fall.   
 
Option #3:  Expand soccer pitch to be a regulation sized pitch, improve drainage and 
resurface with proper aggregate, soil and grass.  The current soccer pitch was 
constructed in 2002 and has served the community well over the years.  
However, it is very limited with its usage as it cannot be used for regulation 
games for teens and adults.  Furthermore, with the increased interest in tackle 
football, ultimate frisbee and flag football the current field cannot be home to 
these growing sports.  Lastly, there is ample room for expansion as the 
underutilized and now under-sized ball field behind the Lou Jeffries arena 
requires a decision from council on it’s future. 
 

Page 3 of 3 
The above three (3) Options would be an increased level of service, with ongoing operating 
and replacement costs.  
 
FINANCIAL CONSIDERATIONS/GRANT OPPORTUNITIES:   
In staff’s review and research of replacing the BMX Track with a Pump Track, and as 
outlined in Council Report REC-2026-08, they have estimated the project cost at 
approximately $500,000. Under the CSRIF 50/50 cost-sharing arrangement, the Town's 
share would therefore be approximately $250,000.  
 
In researching similar sized pickleball courts constructed in central and eastern Ontario the 
past several years, staff estimate a cost of approximately $600,000 for the addition.  Under 
the CSRIF 50/50 cost-sharing arrangement, the Town's share would therefore be 
approximately $300,000. 
 
In staff’s research of expanding and resurfacing the soccer field, staff have estimated the 
project cost at approximately $450,000. Under the CSRIF 50/50 cost-sharing arrangement, 
the Town's share would therefore be approximately $225,000.  
 
Staff are proposing that, if the grant application is successful, the municipal contribution be 
funded from the Town's General Capital Reserves.  The funding for either project would 
need to be considered alongside other corporate capital priorities and demands on available 
reserve balances. 
 
