Council Report – REC-2026-08 
Date: 
October 7, 2026 
☐IN CAMERA
Subject:  
Pump Track Feasibility Report at Site of Old BMX Track 
Author: 
Jeff Johnston, Manager of Parks, Recreation & Facilities☒   OPEN SESSION 
RECOMMENDATION: 
BE IT RESOLVED THAT THE COUNCIL OF THE TOWN OF GANANOQUE RECEIVE 
COUNCIL REPORT REC-2026-08 REGARDING THE FEASIBILITY OF CONVERTING THE 
EXISTING UNUSED BMX TRACK INTO A PUMP TRACK; 
STRATEGIC PLAN COMMENTS: 
Priority #1 – Provide Quality Core Services and Infrastructure – Strategy #2 – Prioritize the 
Implementation of the 10-year Asset Management Plan. 
BACKGROUND: 
On June 3, 2026 a member from the Gananoque community made a presentation before 
council on repurposing the defunct BMX Track and constructing a Pump Track in its place.  
Council passed motion 26-14 directing staff to bring back a report to council with a cost analysis.  
The Town currently has an existing BMX track that is no longer actively used for its original 
purpose. While the site remains a municipal recreational asset, its current condition and 
configuration limit its usefulness to the broader community. 
Staff have reviewed the potential to repurpose the existing location as a modern pump track. A 
pump track is a continuous circuit of berms, rollers and other features that allows users to 
generate momentum through body movement rather than continuous pedalling. A paved pump 
track can accommodate bicycles, BMX bikes, scooters and skateboards and can be designed 
for a range of ages and skill levels. 
Repurposing the existing BMX site may provide advantages over developing a completely new 
recreational site, including the potential reuse of existing grading, earthworks and site 
infrastructure, subject to detailed site assessment. 
INFORMATION/DISCUSSION: 
Existing Site and Preliminary Feasibility 
From a preliminary perspective, the existing BMX track is a logical location to investigate for a 
pump track. Potential advantages include: 

repurposing an underutilized municipal recreation asset;

avoiding the need to acquire or identify a new property;

retaining a wheeled-recreation use at a site historically used for that purpose;

location next to skate-board park;

potential reuse of existing earthworks, grading or site infrastructure;

reducing impacts on other active park uses; and
Report Council-REC-2026-09.1, Attachment 2

Page 2 of 8 
 providing a renewed recreation opportunity without developing previously unused municipal 
land. 
 
The site cannot simply be paved in its current configuration. Pump tracks require specialized 
geometry, drainage, grading, base preparation and pavement construction. A preliminary site 
assessment should confirm drainage, soil and sub-base conditions, existing grades, 
accessibility, site access, surrounding uses, available space and any safety or operational 
considerations. 
 
Community and Recreation Benefits 
A modern pump track would transition the site from a specialized BMX facility into a broader 
multi-user recreation amenity. Potential benefits include outdoor physical activity, youth 
recreation, cycling skill development, use by bicycles, scooters and skateboards, opportunities 
for different ages and abilities, and recreation that does not require scheduled programming or 
staffing to operate. 
 
Construction and Design Considerations 
Based on the Ontario municipal examples reviewed, staff recommend that a permanent asphalt 
facility be the primary option evaluated. Asphalt has a higher initial cost than a basic dirt track 
but provides a consistent riding surface, accommodates a broader range of wheeled users and 
can reduce recurring reshaping and surface maintenance. 
 
Pump-track construction is specialized work. Future design and procurement should require 
demonstrated experience with purpose-built asphalt pump tracks, appropriate base and 
drainage specifications, construction inspection, quality control and warranty provisions. 
 
For preliminary lifecycle planning, a properly designed and constructed asphalt pump track may 
provide a service life of 15 years or more when supported by appropriate base preparation and 
drainage. By comparison, a poorly prepared surface may experience significant deterioration 
within approximately 2-5 years.  
 
Ontario Municipal Comparators 
Staff reviewed several Ontario municipal pump-track projects to understand construction 
approaches, approximate costs, funding strategies and lessons that may be applicable to 
Gananoque. 
 
