Orangeville 
Orangeville provides the most directly comparable example. Its aging dirt and gravel BMX 
facility was selected for conversion to an asphalt pump track, with construction undertaken in 
2025 and corrective asphalt replacement continuing in 2026. The project has a reported capital 
program of approximately $480,000, including a $100,000 contribution from the Orangeville 
Lions Club. Orangeville’s procurement also required demonstrated asphalt pump-track 
construction experience. Deficiencies with the initial asphalt surface later required replacement 
under contractor warranty, reinforcing the importance of base preparation, drainage, 
specifications, quality control and warranty requirements. 
 
Collingwood 
Collingwood completed its beginner/intermediate asphalt pump track at Old Village (Legion) 
Park in 2025. The municipality approved approximately $175,000 for the facility, with the 
completed project reportedly delivered slightly below budget. The example demonstrates that 
project scale and complexity can materially affect cost and that a permanent facility can be 
designed around a more constrained capital target. 
 
 
 
Figure 1 – Collingwood municipal pump track. The aerial view illustrates a compact asphalt facility 
integrated within an existing park setting. Source: Town of Collingwood / municipal project information. 
 
Port Hope 
Port Hope completed its paved pump track at the Town Park Recreation Centre in 2025, with 
the official opening held in November 2025. The project was supported by a $200,000 Ontario 
Trillium Foundation capital grant. The municipality also used community consultation to help 
refine the final design. This demonstrates the potential importance of external capital funding 
and public input. 
 
Loyalist Township 
Loyalist Township began construction of its pump track and skatepark project at Loyalist Park in 
Amherstview in 2026, with the pump track forming Phase One of the development. The overall 
concept has been reported at approximately $1 million and is being phased. A reported 

Page 4 of 8 
$500,000 private donation supports the first phase. Community consultation identified the pump 
track as a priority and also highlighted supporting amenities such as seating and shade.  The 
pump track opened to the public in early August 2026. 
 
 
Figure 2 – Loyalist Township pump track concept at Loyalist Park, Amherstview. The rendering 
demonstrates a larger multi-line facility and integration with surrounding park space. Source: 
Loyalist Township project consultation materials. 
Brockville 
The City of Brockville opened its paved bicycle pump track at Rotary Park in 2015. The City 
identifies the facility as Ontario’s first paved bicycle pump track. The track forms part of a 
broader recreation destination with other park amenities. While a reliable standalone historical 
construction cost has not been identified, Brockville provides a nearby Eastern Ontario example 
demonstrating more than a decade of municipal operation. 
 
Options Assessment 
Staff considered a range of options for the future of the existing BMX site. The comparison 
below combines the site-use alternatives with the feasibility considerations identified through the 
