Staff Report 
 
To:  
 
 
Council  
From:   
 
Roseann Knechtel, Clerk 
Meeting Date:  
November 18, 2026 
Subject:  
 
Council Board Appointments 
______________________________________________________________________ 
Executive Summary: 
This report recommends Council appoint representatives to the Township’s legislated 
boards and committees for a two-year term in accordance with the Township’s Procedural 
By-law. Following a review of board requirements, workload distribution, expertise, 
experience, continuity, potential conflicts of interest, and opportunities for new 
perspectives, staff have developed a recommended slate of appointments. These 
appointments will ensure Council representation on key governance and service delivery 
bodies that support municipal operations and regional partnerships over the next two 
years. 
 
Recommendation: 
That Council appoint the following Council members to sit on the legislated boards and 
committees for a term of two (2) years: 
 
• Mulmur Parks Committee (1): Kim Lyon 
• Mulmur-Melancthon Fire Board (2): Lisa Thompson & Andrew Cunningham 
• Nottawasaga Valley Conservation Authority (1): Janet Horner 
• Police Service Board (1): Andrew Cunningham 
• Provincial Offences (1): Shirley Boxem 
• Rosemont District Fire Board (2): Kim Lyon & Lisa Thompson 
• Shelburne & District Fire Board (2): Janet Horner & Shirley Boxem 
 
Background and Analysis: 
As per the Township’s Procedural By-law, section 39.7, Council appointments shall be for 
a term of 2 years, after which Council will determine whether new members of Council will 
be appointed. 
 
Mulmur Parks Advisory Committee: This committee was renamed from the Manfield 
Parks Committee to the Mulmur Parks Committee in 2026. The Mansfield Parks 
Committee was established in 2010 with the land transfer agreement of the baseball 
diamond to the Township.  The committee has since expanded its mandate to include all 
parklands within Mansfield. This committee meets approximately 4 times a year. 

 
Mulmur-Melancthon Fire Board: A joint board with Melancthon Township administered 
through an agreement date August 11, 2010. It is composed of 2 members from each 
Council. This board meets approximately 4-6 times a year. 
Mulmur-Mono-Melancthon Police Service Board: A joint board with Town of Mono and 
Township of Melancthon which replaced the Mulmur Police Services Board in 2024. It is 
composed of 1 Council member and 1 community member from each municipality plus 1 
provincial appointee. This board meets approximately 4 times a year. 
Nottawasaga Valley Conservation Authority Board of Directors: Currently operating 
as a joint board composed of 1 member from each municipality serviced by the 
Conservation Authority. Appointment to this Board is anticipated to move to the County of 
Dufferin in early 2027 at which point a County Council member would be appointed.  
Provincial Offences Board: A joint board administered under an August 12, 1998 inter-
municipal service agreement. It is composed of 1 representative from each participating 
municipality. Representation varies between the respective municipalities as some have 
Council representation while others have staff representation.  Due to the financial nature 
of the board, it is recommended that the Township Treasurer attend. This board meets 
approximately 4 times a year. 
Rosemont District Fire Board: A joint board with the Town of Mono and Township of 
Adjala-Tosorontio administered through an agreement dated April 18, 2019. It is 
composed of 2 members from each Council. This board meets approximately 4 times a 
year. 
Shelburne & District Fire Board: Currently operating as a joint municipal service board 
with the Towns of Mono and Shelburne and the Townships of Amaranth and Melancthon 
per an agreement dated October 15, 1991. It is composed of 2 members from each 
Council. Potential dissolution of the Board may impact the term of this appointment. This 
board meets approximately 6 times a year. 
Recommendations for board and committee appointments included review of scheduling, 
balancing workloads, areas of expertise, the need for new ideas, and the need for 
consistency (across Fire Boards), experience, ongoing or anticipated matters to be dealt 
with over the next two years and possible conflict of interest. There is also advantages of 
moving representatives to new boards to get new ideas.   
Strategic Plan Alignment: 
It’s in our Nature: Live - We commit to providing a balanced community and providing 
quality services. We will encourage increased community building and respectful social 
interactions to enrich the lives of Mulmur residents. We will provide a range of 
communications and facilities to promote play, growth, connection, active living and 
recognize the changing demographics of Mulmur. 
It’s in our Nature: Work - We commit to fostering a prosperous local economy that serves 
Mulmur’s community and provides for local employment. We will proactively attract new 

 
investment opportunities, and work with Mulmur businesses, industries and institutions to 
retain, expand and enable local opportunities. 
It’s in our Nature: Grow - We commit to developing a framework to shape our Township in 
a manner that protects our agriculture, natural resources and our rural character.  We will 
embrace technology, support energy conservation, climate change mitigation and assess 
environmental impacts in our decision-making process to grow the Township in a 
sustainable manner.   
Financial Impacts: 
Appointed members will receive a per diem amount as outlined in the Township’s Annual 
Allowance for Council By-law 27-2025, except where per diems are provided by the 
legislated body directly. 
 
While the Mayor may attend any meeting as Ex. Officio, clear direction is required where 
the Township directs renumeration.  
 
Environmental Impacts: 
There are no environmental impacts at this time. 
 
 
 
Submitted by: Roseann Knechtel, Clerk 
Approved by: Tracey Atkinson, CAO 
 

 
 
 
 
The Corporation of the Township of Mulmur 
 
By-law No.    - 2026 
 
Being a by-law to confirm the proceedings of the Council of the Corporation of the 
Township of Mulmur for November 18, 2026 
 
 
Whereas Section 5 (1) of the Municipal Act, 2001, as amended, provides that the powers of a 
municipality shall be exercised by Council; 
 
And whereas Section 5 (3) of the Municipal Act, 2001, as amended, provides that municipal 
powers shall be exercised by by-law; 
 
Now therefore the Council of the Corporation of the Township of Mulmur hereby enacts as 
follows: 
 
 
1. All actions of the Council and Committees of Council of the Corporation of the 
Township of Mulmur for the aforementioned date in respect to every report, motion, 
by-law or other action passed and taken by Council or Committees of Council, 
including the exercise of natural person powers, are hereby adopted, ratified and 
