A Briefing Note contains advice and/or recommendations from an employee, for council consideration. 
 
SUBMITTED BY 
 
Joel Stapley – Treasurer 
 
 
DATE 
 
November 17, 2026 
 
BACKGROUND The Town of Moosonee provides water and wastewater services that are funded in 
part through user fees based on water consumption. 
Accurate billing depends on properly functioning water meters installed at serviced 
properties. 
Over time, a number of water meters have become damaged, inactive, inaccessible, 
or otherwise unable to provide accurate consumption readings. Town staff estimate 
that approximately 100 properties are currently not being billed based on actual water 
use due to meter issues. 
To address this issue, the Town intends to undertake a water meter replacement and 
compliance program to ensure all serviced properties have working meters and are 
billed fairly and accurately. 
 
ANALYSIS & 
DISCUSSION FOR 
COUNCIL 
CONSIDERATION 
The absence of functioning water meters creates inequities within the water billing 
system. 
Property owners with functioning meters are billed based on actual consumption, 
while properties with inactive or non-functioning meters may not be contributing their 
fair share toward water and wastewater service costs. 
To support the Town's efforts to replace and maintain water meters, staff recommend 
adding a new fee to the Fees and Services By-law. 
The fee would apply to properties where a water meter is inactive, damaged, or where 
access for replacement, inspection, repair, or installation is not provided within a 
reasonable period of notice from the Town. 
The proposed fee is intended to encourage compliance with meter replacement 
requirements and ensure the Town can recover a portion of the costs associated with 
providing water services where accurate consumption data cannot be obtained. 
 
RELEVANT POLICY 
AND/OR 
LEGISLATION 
 
 
• By-Law 14-26 – Water and Sewer Rates By-Law 
 
FINANCIAL 
IMPLICATIONS 
Accurate water metering is essential for ensuring users are billed fairly and that the 
Town recovers costs associated with delivering water and wastewater services. 
The implementation of a compliance fee will support the Town's efforts to improve 
billing accuracy and reduce revenue losses associated with inactive or non-functioning 
water meters. 
BRIEFING NOTE 
Water Meter Replacement Program 
 

 
ALIGN WITH 
COUNCIL 
STRATEGIC PLAN 
PRIORITIES 
 
- 
• Promoting efficient and sustainable service delivery. 
• Ensuring infrastructure is properly maintained and managed. 
• Supporting financial sustainability through fair and equitable user-pay 
systems. 
RECOMMENDATIONS  That Council approve an amendment to By-Law 14-26, Water and Sewer Rates By-
Law, to establish a Non-Compliance / Inactive Water Meter Fee of $250.00 per month 
for properties with inactive, damaged, inaccessible, or non-compliant water meters. 
 
CAO’S COMMENTS 
AND/OR MOTION 
FOR COUNCIL 
CONSIDERATION 
That Council approve an amendment to By-Law 14-26, Water and Sewer Rates 
By-Law, establishing a Non-Compliance / Inactive Water Meter Fee of $250.00 
per month, effective immediately or on a date determined by Council. 
 
 

  
 
 
 
 
 
A Briefing Note contains advice and/or recommendations from an employee, for council consideration. 
SUBMITTED BY Todd Francis – Interim Public Works Manager 
DATE November 17th, 2026 
 
BACKGROUND The Town of Moosonee Motor Grader TA1 (Caterpillar 140H) is a critical 
asset used for road maintenance and winter operations.  
A recent inspection by Toromont Cat identified deficiencies, including safety-
related items that must be addressed before the unit can be returned to 
service.  
The grader currently has approximately 17,838 operating hours and has not 
historically benefited from a structured preventative maintenance program. 
Maintenance has generally been reactive, with repairs completed when 
failures occurred.  
The proposed repairs are intended to address immediate deficiencies and 
extend the useful life of the equipment. 
 
ANALYSIS & 
DISCUSSION FOR 
COUNCIL 
CONSIDERATION 
The recommended repairs will return the grader to safe and reliable 
operation and extend the service life of this critical asset.  
Additional preventative maintenance and repairs will likely be required in 
future years to maximize its remaining useful life.  
The cost of the required repairs is $62,409.70 including HST.  
Replacement of the grader with a comparable unit is estimated between 
$700,000 and $800,000 and is not financially feasible currently.  
While the grader is being repaired, Public Works can temporarily utilize the 
Airport Grader; however, access to that equipment is limited and cannot 
support long-term operational needs.  
Timely approval of these repairs is imperative.  
Long-term replacement planning will be addressed through the 2027 Budget 
and the Towns 5-10 Year Financial Plan, which will include the replacement 
date for this unit. 
 
RELEVANT POLICY 
AND/OR 
LEGISLATION 
Purchasing Policy.  
This is a sole-source procurement from Toromont Cat, the authorized 
Caterpillar dealer that completed the inspection and provided the repair 
quotation. 
 
FINANCIAL 
IMPLICATIONS 
The repair cost of $62,409.70 including HST will be funded partially from the 
Operating Budget, with $ 54,000 coming from 1-4-3043-2010, while the 
remaining $ 8,409.70 will be funded from reserves. 
 
BRIEFING NOTE 
Grader Repairs (Unit TA1) 
 

ALIGN WITH 
COUNCIL STRATEGIC 
PLAN PRIORITIES 
 
Supports Council’s priorities for road maintenance, winter operations, 
safety, and asset management. 
RECOMMENDATION
S  
That Council approve the sole-source repair of Motor Grader TA1 by 
Toromont Cat in the amount of $62,409.70 including HST with funding 
coming from the Operating Budget, with $ 54,000 coming from 1-4-3043-
2010, while the remaining $ 8,409.70 will be funded from reserves. 
 
CAO’S COMMENTS 
AND/OR MOTION 
