A Briefing Note contains advice and/or recommendations from an employee, for council consideration. 
SUBMITTED BY Nariman Emadpoor - Public Works Manager 
 
DATE September 9, 2026 
 
BACKGROUND Public Works requires a snowblower attachment for the Town’s Trackless municipal 
tractor to clear sidewalks, fire hydrants, building entrances and other confined areas. 
The complete truck loading chute is included so that, where snow cannot be cast, the 
operator can discharge directly into a haul truck alongside the machine. 
Joe Johnson Equipment Inc. (JJEI) provided a written quotation dated September 8, 
2026 for the snowblower with sidewalk chute, the complete truck loading chute, and 
freight to Moosonee. Only one quotation was obtained, for the reasons set out below. 
 
ANALYSIS & 
DISCUSSION FOR 
COUNCIL 
CONSIDERATION 
The attachments are manufactured by Trackless Vehicles Ltd. and are proprietary to 
the Trackless tractor the Town already operates. They mount on the machine’s quick-
change pins and run off its PTO and cab hydraulics. No other manufacturer makes a 
compatible attachment. 
Joe Johnson Equipment Inc. carries Trackless equipment, attachments and parts 
exclusively for this territory. There is no other supplier the Town can obtain Trackless 
product from, and therefore no second or third quotation available for these goods. 
On that basis the purchase is a Sole Source under Section 5.1 of the Procurement 
Policy: specialized goods not available from multiple suppliers, protected by exclusive 
distribution rights, and required for compatibility with existing Town equipment where 
no alternative exists. Obtaining the competitive quotations the policy normally calls for 
is not possible, and this is not a case of avoiding competition. 
 
RELEVANT POLICY 
AND/OR 
LEGISLATION 
Town of Moosonee Procurement Policy, By-law No. 01-2026 - Section 5.1 (Sole 
Source Procurement) and Section 6.3 (Non-Competitive Procurement Approvals). 
 
FINANCIAL 
IMPLICATIONS 
 Snowblower with sidewalk chute: $14,000.00 
 Truck loading chute complete: $2,925.00 
 Freight to Moosonee: $1,800.00 
 Total: $18,725.00 exclusive of HST 
Excluding HST and freight, the value of the goods is $16,925.00, which requires 
Council approval on the recommendation of the CAO. To be funded from the 2026 
Public Works equipment budget, with availability confirmed by the Treasurer before a 
purchase order is issued. 
A 16” chute extension was also quoted at $911.67 and is not recommended at this 
time. The quotation is stated to be valid for seven days, so pricing is to be confirmed 
in writing with JJEI when the purchase order is issued. 
 
 
BRIEFING NOTE 
Sole Source Purchase of Trackless Snowblower and Truck Loading 
Chute 

ALIGN WITH 
COUNCIL 
STRATEGIC PLAN 
PRIORITIES 
 
 
Public safety - winter access to fire hydrants and pedestrian routes. 
 
Accessibility - sidewalks and pedestrian routes cleared to a consistent 
standard. 
 
Responsible asset management - added capability on a machine the Town 
already owns rather than a new unit. 
 
RECOMMENDATIONS  That Council approve the sole source purchase of one (1) Trackless 60” ribbon 
snowblower with sidewalk chute and complete truck loading chute from Joe Johnson 
Equipment Inc., at a total cost of $18,725.00 exclusive of HST and inclusive of freight 
to Moosonee, in accordance with Sections 5.1 and 6.3 of the Procurement Policy; and 
that the sole source justification and supporting quotation be filed with the Treasurer. 
 
CAO’S COMMENTS 
AND/OR MOTION 
FOR COUNCIL 
CONSIDERATION 
BE IT RESOLVED THAT the Council of the Corporation of the Town of Moosonee 
approves the purchase of a Trackless 60” Ribbon Snowblower in the amount of 
$18,275.00, plus applicable HST, for a total purchase price of $20,650.75. 
 
 

  
 
 
 
 
 
A Briefing Note contains advice and/or recommendations from an employee, for council consideration. 
 
 
SUBMITTED BY 
 
 
 
 
Boblin Jardino – Municipal Clerk 
 
DATE 
 
 
September 22, 2026 
 
 
 
      BACKGROUND 
 
 
   
 
To seek Council approval to amend the October 2026 regular meeting sched-
ule by cancelling the October 20, 2026 Regular Council Meeting and main-
taining only one Regular Council Meeting on October 6, 2026. 
The Town of Moosonee Procedural By-law establishes the regular Council 
meeting schedule and provides that Council will generally hold two regular 
meetings per month, with exceptions in July, August, and December. 
Administration notes that the current Council will be operating under the 
restricted authority provisions of the Municipal Act, commonly referred to as 
“Lame Duck” status, following the 2026 municipal election period. 
 
 
 
ANALYSIS & 
DISCUSSION FOR 
COUNCIL 
CONSIDERATION 
 
 
 
Given the upcoming municipal election and the limited authority available to 
Council while in restricted authority (Lame Duck) status, Administration 
recommends reducing the October 2026 meeting schedule to one Regular 
Council Meeting. The Regular Council Meeting scheduled for Tuesday, 
October 6, 2026 would proceed as planned. The Regular Council Meeting 
scheduled for Tuesday, October 20, 2026 would be cancelled. This approach 
aligns the meeting schedule with operational requirements during the 
election period while maintaining Council’s ability to address necessary 
municipal business. 
 
 
 
RELEVANT POLICY 
AND/OR 
LEGISLATION 
 
 
 
The Procedural By-law provides that Council shall hold two Regular Council 
Meetings per month, except during July, August, and December. Council may 
amend its meeting schedule by resolution. 
 
 
 
 
BRIEFING NOTE 
October 2026 Council Meeting Schedule 
 

 
 
 
FINANCIAL 
IMPLICATIONS 
 
 
 
 
 
There are no direct financial implications associated with this 
recommendation. 
 
 
 
ALIGN WITH 
COUNCIL 
STRATEGIC PLAN 
PRIORITIES 
 
 
 
 
Supports effective governance, efficient municipal administration, and an 
orderly transition to the newly constituted Council following the 2026 
municipal election. 
  
 
 
RECOMMENDATIONS 
 
 
  
 
THAT Council approves a modification to the October 2026 Regular Council 
Meeting schedule; 
 
AND THAT the Regular Council Meeting scheduled for October 20, 2026, be 
cancelled; 
 
AND THAT the next Regular Council Meeting following October 6, 2026 be 
held by the newly constituted Council in accordance with the Procedural By-
law and applicable legislative requirements. 
 
 
 
CAO’S COMMENTS 
 AND/OR MOTION 
FOR COUNCIL 
CONSIDERATION 
 
 
 
BE IT RESOLVED THAT the Council of the Corporation of the Town of 
Moosonee approves the recommendation outlined in the Briefing Note, as 
supported by the Chief Administrative Officer. 
 
 

 
A Briefing Note contains advice and/or recommendations from an employee, for council consideration. 
 
SUBMITTED BY 
 
 
Terah Racine – Manager of CDWCE 
 
DATE 
 
 
September 14, 2026 
 
 
 
      BACKGROUND 
 
 
   
   As part of the 2026–2027 arena season, the Town of Moosonee is introducing 
expanded advertising opportunities, including in-ice and dasher-board advertising. 
   The proposed MMHA centre-ice logo requires two large Easy In Textile logo spaces 
at $3,500 each, for a total value of $7,000. 
   Recognizing the opportunity to support youth hockey fundraising, Recreation offered 
Moosonee Minor Hockey Association (MMHA) an arena advertising partnership. 
Under the proposed model, MMHA would assist with selling available in-ice and 
dasher-board advertising. The first $7,000 in advertising sales generated through 
MMHA's efforts would be credited toward the cost of the MMHA centre-ice logo. Any 
additional revenue generated through MMHA's sales would be shared 50/50 between 
the Town and MMHA. 
   The opportunity was presented with the understanding that Recreation already has 
a number of businesses with logos and interest in arena advertising. These 
opportunities had the potential to cover the cost of the MMHA centre-ice logo and 
generate additional fundraising revenue for the association. 
   MMHA has since advised that they have decided not to proceed with the proposed 
advertising partnership. 
 
 
ANALYSIS & 
DISCUSSION FOR 
COUNCIL 
CONSIDERATION 
 
 
   As MMHA has chosen not to proceed with the advertising partnership, Council 
direction is required regarding the two large centre-ice advertising spaces proposed 
for MMHA. 
  Council may consider: 
1. MMHA purchases the space – MMHA pays the $7,000 advertising value. 
2. Council approves a full or partial donation – Council waives all or a portion 
of the $7,000 value in accordance with the Town's Donation Policy. 
3. The space is made available to another advertiser – If Council does not 
approve a donation and MMHA does not wish to purchase the space, 
Recreation will seek another business or organization interested in 
purchasing the advertising opportunity. 
 
RELEVANT POLICY 
AND/OR 
LEGISLATION 
 Town of Moosonee Municipal Donations Policy 
BRIEFING NOTE 
MMHA Centre-Ice Advertising Space 

 
 
FINANCIAL 
IMPLICATIONS 
 
The total value of the two large centre-ice advertising spaces is $7,000. 
 
MMHA purchase: $7,000 in advertising revenue. 
 
Full or partial donation: Foregone advertising revenue equal to the value 
approved by Council. 
 
Alternative advertiser: Potential advertising revenue of up to $7,000. 
 
ALIGN WITH 
COUNCIL 
STRATEGIC PLAN 
PRIORITIES 
 
This initiative supports Council's priorities by: 
 
Supporting community recreation and youth programming through 
opportunities that contribute to the sustainability of local minor hockey.  
 
Strengthening community partnerships by exploring collaborative 
approaches between the Town and local organizations.  
 
Supporting financial sustainability by maximizing available arena 
advertising opportunities and generating revenue to support recreation 
operations. 
  
 
RECOMMENDATIONS 
 
  
  That Council provide direction regarding the two large centre-ice advertising spaces 
proposed for Moosonee Minor Hockey Association for the 2026–2027 arena season. 
   Options for consideration include requiring MMHA to purchase the space at a cost of 
$7,000, approving a full or partial donation in accordance with the Town's Donation 
Policy, or directing Recreation to make the advertising space available to another 
business or organization. 
 
 
 
CAO’S COMMENTS 
 AND/OR MOTION 
FOR COUNCIL 
CONSIDERATION 
 
 
MOTION FOR COUNCIL’S CONSIDERATION: 
 
 
That Town Council approve requiring MMHA to purchase the space at a cost of 
$ 7,000. 
 

