Staff Reports 
 
H. Gropp- CAO 
Landfill MOU-  
As previously reported, there was a regional landfill meeting on the 29th of July. The purpose of 
the meeting was to discuss regional collaboration on landfill management and issues facing the 
reason. As a result of the meeting, it was agreed that all communities would work together on a 
regional solution. The expansion of the Sioux Narrows Landfill was identified as the preferred 
option. From this meeting, it was agreed that all communities would enter into a memorandum 
of understanding. One has been prepared, and we are seeking Council Authorization to have the 
CAO and Mayor sign the agreement. 
 
Letter of Support-Onigaming –  
As part of this process, the Community of Onigaming requested that the Township provide a 
letter of support to the Province, for their interest in expanding their waste management capacity 
on Peterson Road.  
 
Landfill Services Contract- 
As the Township moves towards Landfill Expansion, we requested a proposal from Stantec 
Engineering to continue their work with us on the next phase of the file. It is recommended that 
we proceed with Stantec as they have been supporting the Township through this project so far 
and it is best value to have them continue to support the project. This is an unbudgeted amount, 
but this expense is eligible in the new allocation of NORDS funding that the Township  
 

Council Minutes-October 6, 2026 – Page 3 
 
was awarded in the spring. Alternatively, funding for this contract could be taken from the 
landfill. 
 
M.Hanson- Treasurer/Clerk 
Tax Sale Update – 
Four tax sales were scheduled on September 16, 2026. Property owners for three of the four 
sales paid all arrears before the tax sale date, and the tax sale certificates were cancelled on those 
three properties.  The fourth property did not garner a bid ahead of the tax sale date/time 
deadline.  However, the Township did receive a bid two days after the sale date/time, and have 
received one telephone request from a person who intended to bid but was out of town and did 
not meet the deadline.  Unfortunately, the Township was not able to accept the late bid.  Both 
parties indicated they are willing to purchase the property from the municipality if possible. This 
property is a remote (no access road) mining claim property (vacant land).  Most enquiries  
received ahead of the tax sale were in regard to mineral rights. 
We do have options for this property as outlined by Realtax. 
 
Council deferred any decisions regarding the property until more information, such as the MPAC 
assessed value, can be given at the next meeting of Council, October 20, 2026. 
M. Hanson will report back to Council at the October 20, 2026 meeting. 
 
M.Kakeeway- Fire Chief  
Training and Professional Development- 
Fire Chief M. Kakeeway attending FireCon in Thunder Bay, September 14th-19th to complete the 
NFPA 1041 Fire Service Instructor II course. 
 
The department continues to focus on maintaining and improving firefighter readiness through 
regular weekly training. The knowledge gained through the NFPA 1041 Instructor II course will 
be used to help develop more structured training sessions, practical scenarios, and evaluations 
for members. This will allow the department to better identify training needs, track member 
progress, and ensure our firefighters are prepared to respond safely and effectively to the variety 
of emergencies within our community. 
 
Emergency Response- 
During September, the department responded to two calls involving downed hydro lines. These 
calls have become more frequent this year, making it important that firefighters remain familiar 
with the hazards and appropriate safety procedures associated with these incidents.  
As of the end of September, the department has responded to 41 calls for service year-to-date. 
 
 
 
 

Council Minutes-October 6, 2026 – Page 4 
 
R.Stelter-Director of Community Development 
Time Change Survey- 
On Sept. 17, Manitoba announced its intention to remain on permanent daylight saving time. As 
a result, many communities within the northwest released surveys asking residents what their 
preference was. On Sept. 21, the Township released a survey that was posted to the Township’s 
website, shared on social media and emailed to local businesses.  
 
As of Sept. 29, the survey received 157 responses and demonstrated overwhelming support for 
northwestern Ontario to adopt permanent daylight time and remain aligned with Manitoba. A 
total of 147 respondents (93.6%) supported this option, while five respondents (3.2%) preferred 
permanent standard time and four respondents (2.5%) preferred continuing the current practice 
of changing clocks twice a year. Most responses came from residents of the Sioux Narrows-
Nestor Falls area, with additional input from surrounding communities and Manitoba. 
 
During a recent regional discussion with Minister Greg Rickford, representatives indicated that 
surveys conducted by other municipalities, including Kenora, Dryden, and Fort Frances, also 
showed strong support, generally exceeding 90 percent, for aligning with Manitoba. 
Municipalities have been asked to provide formal resolutions or position statements to the 
province outlining their preferred approach. The discussion also highlighted potential 
operational concerns for health care providers if neighbouring communities were to operate 
under different time systems, reinforcing the importance of a consistent regional approach. 
 
Rural Ontario Development Program- 
The Township is applying for a provincial grant to help fund the creation of a new economic 
development strategy. The last one was completed in 2022 and is 80% completed. The Rural 
Ontario Development (ROD) Program is a provincial funding program that supports projects that 
strengthen rural economies, attract investment, retain and create jobs, and enhance community 
assets. Municipalities are eligible to apply for cost-shared funding under the Strategies and Plans 
stream, which provides up to 50% of eligible project costs to a maximum of $50,000 for the 
development of economic development strategies and plans. The proposed Economic 
Development Strategy will help identify opportunities for business attraction, retention and 
expansion, support long-term economic growth, and position the Township to pursue future 
investment and funding opportunities. 
 
Green Municipal Fund-  
The Green Municipal Fund (GMF) Rapid Adaptation Projects: Adaptation in Action program 
provides funding to help smaller municipalities implement practical projects that reduce climate 
related risks and improve community resilience. Municipalities with populations under 50,000 
are eligible to apply for grants covering 70% to 100% of eligible project costs, up to a maximum 
of $75,000, for projects that address climate impacts such as extreme heat, flooding, wildfire risk, 
and the resilience of community facilities. The proposed heating and cooling system upgrade at  

Council Minutes-October 6, 2026 – Page 5 
 
the Northern Ontario Sportfishing Centre would improve the facility's ability to remain safe, 
comfortable, and operational during periods of extreme temperatures, while enhancing the long-
term resilience of this important municipal asset. 
 
Community Safety and Policing Grant-  
The municipality of Sioux Narrows-Nestor Falls in partnership with the Kenora OPP Detachment, 
is seeking funding through the Community Safety and Policing Grant to establish a CCTV network 
with Automated License Plate Recognition (ALPR) capability at strategic locations throughout 
the municipality. This project will enhance community safety by supporting the prevention, 
detection, and investigation of criminal activity, including the trafficking of drugs, firearms, and 
persons along the Highway 71 corridor. 
A grant application has been submitted for a total of $185,500 over 3 years. 
 
Community Emergency Preparedness Grant-  
We will be seeking a $50,000 grant to improve emergency preparedness in our community 
through the acquisition of increased communication equipment for the Fire Department. We will 
also be allocating a portion of this grant to upgrade our HIIRA (Hazards identification) and 
include community engagement and awareness emergency awareness and the hazards most 
relevant to our region. 
 
Community Wildland Fire Protection Grant-  
We will be seeking a $15,000 grant to obtain forest fire hazard mapping for our community. 
 
Bass Lake Park Upgrades-  
Work on the upgrades to Bass Lake Park began Monday, Sept. 26, 2026. Township staff attended 
the site to see some of the early progress. The park is closed to the public while contractors work 
to improve the playground, expand the beach area and create a more welcoming park for 
residents and visitors to enjoy. Work is expected to be completed later this fall. 
Visitor Numbers 2026- 
Visitor activity remained strong throughout both information centres during the 2026 summer 
season. Between May and August, the Northern Ontario Sportfishing Centre (NOSFC) recorded 
8,778 walk-ins, up 4.6% from 2025. July and August were the busiest months, with August 
recording 3,491 visitors. NOSFC hosted 30+ events and activities from mid-May through 
September, including five music concerts, workshops, Kids Fishing for Fun, educational 
presentations and children's programming. Two August long weekend concerts attracted 600+ 
guests. 
The Nestor Falls Information Centre recorded 3,163 visitor interactions between May and 
August with overall traffic remaining comparable to 2025. Information inquiries increased, with 
more local residents stopping in for maps, guides and local information. 
 
 

