Delegations- All Nations Health Partners -Ontario Health Team  
- Community Patient Advisory Council: Strengthening the Northern Health Travel Grant 
-Kim Elke presented to Council on the initiative to make changes and improvements to the 
Northern Travel Heath Grant.  
She explained that for many in Northern Ontario, patients face many unavoidable barriers to 
access healthcare. Travel is essential to access certain medical care, and the current Northern 
Ontario Health Grant reimbursement levels do not accurately reflect northern travel costs, which 
results in out-of-pockets costs. Difficulties navigating the application process, costs and lack of 
resources lead to delayed or missed appointments, further burdening the healthcare system. 
Based on identified barriers, the Community Patient Advisory Council has proposed short term, 
medium term and long-term goals to address the financial, administrative and structural barriers 
over time.  
The goals are as follows:  
 
SHORT-TERM (0–6 MONTHS): REVIEW REIMBURSEMENT LEVELS 
Review and consider increasing Northern Health Travel Grant per-kilometre rate, recognizing the increased 
likelihood of overnight stays and better reflect higher out-of-pocket costs, including fuel consumption, vehicle 
wear, and winter driving conditions in Northern Ontario. 
Increase reimbursement caps, including length of stay restrictions, for medically required overnight travel, 
where same-day return is not feasible due to distance, availability of transportation or appointment 
scheduling. 
Prioritize changes that can be implemented within existing administrative structures, with the objective of 
reducing immediate financial barriers to accessing medically necessary care. 
 
MEDIUM-TERM (6–12 MONTHS): IMPROVE NAVIGATION SUPPORT 
Pilot patient navigation support to assist with NHTG applications. 
Prioritize support for patients who are most vulnerable or require escorts, including strong linkages for First 
Nations and Indigenous communities. 
Focus on assistance with existing eligibility and documentation requirements, not on expanding eligibility. 
 
LONG-TERM (12-18 MONTHS): FORMALIZE PATIENT INPUT 
Establish a patient advisory mechanism specific to NHTG. 
Ensure patient perspectives inform ongoing program evaluation and refinement. 
Align with Ontario Health principles of patient partnership. 
 
Discussion:  
Council expressed gratitude over the initiative, highlighting that this is an issue that affects 
everyone. The Northern Health Travel Grant does not accurately reflect reimbursement levels,  

Council Minutes- September 1, 2026-Page 3 
 
and so many people do not have the support from family or friends to mitigate many of the 
barriers that come with having to travel for healthcare. 
One Councilor asked if assistance would be based on income level, and Kim clarified that it would 
be needs based, not income based. It was asked if cross-provincial travel was being considered, 
as going to Winnipeg would solve so many problems. Kim explained that going to Winnipeg does 
not solve all the problems, and really only benefits those in Kenora and the immediate area. It 
does not benefit everyone in the North, and does not change the complexity of travel. 
Kim noted that there is an upcoming virtual Northern Health Travel Initiative on September 9, 
2026 and will share with Council for those that would like to attend. 
 
