Staff Reports 
 
H. Gropp –CAO– report attached.  On behalf of M. Kakeeway- Fire Chief 
Burn Complaints/ Fire Ban Enforcement: 
 The fire department responded to four complaints involving campfires during the Restricted 
Fire Zone and Municipal Fire Ban, all of which were located at Caliper Lake Provincial Park. They 
will continue to Montier and respond to reports of burning during fire restrictions, while 
working with the MNRF and park staff to promote compliance and public safety.  
Nestor Falls Equipment: 
The Nestor Falls Fire Station has a new-to-us Hurst battery powered hydraulic cutters. This gives 
the department added capability during motor vehicle collisions and other technical rescue 
operations.  
Dunk Tank and Grease Fire Demonstration: 
The dunk tank fundraiser on August 11, raised approximately $150. A grease fire demonstration 
was also conducted to educate the public on what to do, and what not to do when faced with a 
cooking grease fire. 
Sioux Narrows Men’s Club Donation: 
The Sioux Narrows Men’s Club graciously donated four Mustang life jackets for use on the fire 
boats. This is a valuable addition to the water rescue equipment and will provide an added level 
of protection for firefighters, guests, paramedics when operating on the water. 
Sioux Narrows-Nestor Falls Fire By-Law and Code of Conduct: 
Lobstik solutions conducted a benchmarking study of the fire department to determine how it 
compared to similar departments regarding levels of service, training hour requirements, and 
renumeration. Based on the study, staff are recommending changes to the Fire and Rescue 
Services Code of Conduct Policy H-01.  
These changes include: 
-Increasing mandatory training hours from 24 hours to 50 hours per year.  
-Permitting hours in public service initiatives to count towards hours.  
-Increasing the rate of pay for certified members from $20/hour to $25/hour  
 

Council Minutes- September 1, 2026-Page 5 
 
General discussion was had, and Council was in agreement of the recommended changes. 
 
R.Stelter-Director of Community Development 
Several upcoming provincial and federal funding opportunities align with current Township 
priorities. Four programs have been identified that may support improvements to municipal 
facilities, recreation infrastructure, housing- enabling infrastructure and economic development 
planning. 
Green Municipal Fund 
The Federation of Canadian Municipalities (FCM) is accepting applications for its Rapid 
Adaptation Projects program between October 6 and November 10, 2026. The program is 
intended for small and rural municipalities to implement projects that address climate risks and 
improve community resilience. Grants are available for up to $75,000 and may cover between 
70% and 100% of eligible costs depending on municipal eligibility. 
Staff will be pursuing funding to address the ongoing heating and cooling challenges at the 
Northern Ontario Sport Fishing Centre. 
Community Sport and Recreation Infrastructure Fund 
The Province of Ontario established the Community Sport and Recreation Infrastructure Fund to 
support the repair, upgrading, and development of recreation facilities across Ontario 
communities. The program was created through a provincial investment of $200 million to 
improve and expand community sport and recreation infrastructure. 
Staff would pursue funding for recreation improvements within Nestor Falls, including upgrades 
to the Nestor Falls Community Rink and expansion of the community trail network, with focus 
on the Red Pine Trail. 
Municipal Housing Infrastructure Program- Non-Development Charge Municipalities Stream 
The Governments of Ontario and Canada recently announced a new $1 billion funding initiative 
to assist municipalities that do not levy development charges with the construction of housing-
enabling infrastructure. The new Non-Development Charge Municipalities Stream is intended to 
support infrastructure such as roads, bridges, water systems, and other municipal projects that 
facilitate residential development. Applications are scheduled to open October 29, 2026. 
This funding is critical to enabling future residential growth within the Township and would be 
used a Granite Ridge access road, removing a key barrier to future residential development. 
Previous applications through FedNor were unsuccessful. 
Rural Ontario Development Program 
Ontario's Rural Ontario Development Program provides cost-shared funding to municipalities 
and rural organizations undertaking projects that strengthen local economies, attract 
investment, and build community capacity. The program includes a funding stream specifically  
supporting economic development strategies and planning initiatives, offering up to 50% cost-
share funding to a maximum of $50,000. 
Staff would apply for this funding to be used to develop a new Economic Development Strategy 
for the Township. The current strategy was developed in 2022 and would benefit from review 
and updating to reflect current economic conditions, housing opportunities, labour market 
trends, tourism development priorities, and infrastructure needs. A new strategy would provide  

Council Minutes- September 1, 2026-Page 6 
 
updated direction for attracting investment, supporting local businesses, growing the residential 
tax base, and advancing economic development priorities over the coming years. 
 
Nestor Falls Government Dock Review 
The review of the Nestor Falls government dock is still underway. Some solutions have been 
identified, but staff are continuing to conduct research and are hopeful to have a full review 
completed by the October Council meeting. 
 
Council expressed optimism with the funding streams and the projects selected. Some general 
discussion was had regarding current Township projects. Other possible projects for funding 
opportunities were identified (such as the CSRIF being used for ends on the Sioux Narrows Four 
Season Recreation Facility) but staff clarified that they would not be able to submit multiple 
applications and would have to decide where the greater need would be. 
It was asked if any of the grants would cover the cleaning of the airstrip and staff did not think 
any of the current funding would. 
 
