NOTICE OF MOTION 

Council Minutes - October 06, 2026 
 
 
NEW BUSINESS 
911 Signs 
 
Mayor Mayberry informed Council about noticing some 911 signs across the Township 
that are damaged, missing or placed in a less visible location. He commented on the 
importance of the visibility of 911 signs for emergency services and requested some 
educational campaigns in Township newsletters and social media pages advising 
people of the visibility requirements for 911 signs. 
  
 
COUNCIL ROUND TABLE 
Council members shared updates from their respective wards and community 
involvement. 
  
Councillor Pickard commented on the car show that took place the previous weekend 
organized by the Sweaburg Lions Club where 64 cars attended. He mentioned the 
upcoming Hike for Hunger and Health walk being hosted by the Township Engage and 
Inform Committee on October 17th at Trillium Woods. 
  
Councillor Ypma advised he had attended a Long Point Region Conservation Authority 
meeting the previous Tuesday where they set their 2027 budget, with a decrease of 
2.3% to their general levy. Dereham Centre Hall Board has started the removal of the 
mulch at the playground with four loads having been removed so far. 
  
Councillor Klosler reported that attendance at the recent Cornhole Tournament held at 
the Mount Elgin Community Centre was slightly lower than at the previous event. He 
also noted that a Trivia Night is scheduled for November 13. Councillor Klosler advised 
that residents have expressed significant concerns regarding local road safety in light of 
the recent number of accidents. He further reported that he attended the Aging Well 
Roadshow at the Salford Community Centre, which unfortunately had low attendance; 
and he noted that the Dereham Centre Lions Club has welcomed three new members, 
who are eager to support fundraising efforts for the new Mount Elgin Community Park. 
  
Councillor Gillis reported that he also attended the Aging Well Roadshow and noted that 
attendance at the event was unfortunately low. He further advised that the Beachville 
Museum hosted its final car show of 2026, which drew record attendance of more than 
300 vehicles; and he noted that the Beachville Museum Board will meet the following 
week to discuss its 2027 budget, and that the Beachville Parks and Recreation 
Committee will also be meeting to consider its 2027 budget. 
  
Councillor Buchner informed Council that the Engage and Inform Committee was 
meeting the following day and reminded Council of the upcoming Hike for Hunger and 
Health put on the by the Committee at Trillium Woods on October 17th. He added he 

Council Minutes - October 06, 2026 
would be attending a Brownsville Hall Board meeting the following night where they will 
be working on their 2027 budget. 
  
Councillor Way informed Council there was a Folden's Hall Board meeting on 
September 16th where they approved their 2027 budget for presentation to Council; and 
that he attended a Beachville Museum Board meeting on September 23rd where the 
2027 budget was presented. He added the next Folden's Hall Board meeting would take 
place on November 3rd. 
  
Mayor Mayberry highlighted the plan for the Township's 2027 budget where distribution 
is expected during the last week of November, and deliberations and amendments 
would begin at the first meeting in December. It's expected the budget will be adopted at 
the first meeting in January of 2027. Similarly to years past, there will be an opportunity 
for all hall boards to present their budgets to Council starting in December.   
  
  
