Prince Township Clerk’s Council Report: November 2026 
Note: 
   Regular Council Meeting 
Subject:                                 Clerk’s Report on Ongoing projects and Updates 
Prepared by: 
 
     Megha Trivedi, Clerk - Treasurer 
Purpose 
The purpose of this report is to provide Council with an update on current administrative matters, 
projects, and activities undertaken since the previous regular meeting of Council. 
 
1. 2026 Municipal Election 
The 2026 Municipal Election was successfully completed on October 26, 2026. All voting activities, 
including Advance Voting Day on October 14, 2026, and Voting Day on October 26, 2026, have 
concluded. 
Administration is completing the remaining post-election administrative requirements and 
preparing for the transition to the 2026–2030 term of Council. 
2. Antique Farm Shed – Demolition Update 
The demolition of the Antique Farm Shed has been substantially completed. 
Following a review of the site and the Township's equipment storage requirements, the existing 
concrete pad will be retained and utilized for storing equipment at the same location. 
As the concrete pad will remain in place, the contractor will adjust the final invoice accordingly to 
reflect the revised scope of work. 
3. Deans Road Park – Project Completion 
The Deans Road Park project has been completed. 
The completion of this project represents the conclusion of the planned improvements at the park. 
4. Winter Road Maintenance – Sander Purchase 
As part of preparations for the upcoming winter maintenance season, the Public Works Department 
has identified the need to purchase a sander to support winter road maintenance operations. 

Funding for this equipment was included and approved in the Township's 2026 municipal budget. 
However, as the proposed purchase exceeds the spending authority established under the 
Township's procurement bylaw, the quotation is being brought forward to Council for formal 
approval prior to proceeding with the purchase. 
The quotation is included in the Council agenda package for consideration. 
Submitted respectfully, 
Megha Trivedi 
Clerk-Treasurer 

Council Date:  November 3, 2026 
  
Attention:  ​
Mayor Melanie Mageran, Prince Township Council 
RE:​
​
Minor Variance Application M2-2026; Consent Application C2-2026 
Address: ​
2866 Base Line  
 
Report By: Steve Turco, RPP - Borealis Planning Services 
Purpose: 
The purpose of this report is to recommend a severance to facilitate the creation of 2 new 
lots, as well as associated minor variances in support of the requested severances. 
Analysis: 
The subject property is located at 2866 Base Line, approximately 250 metres east of Walls 
Side Road. The subject property is approximately 2.54 ha (6.29 acres), with an approximate 
frontage of 126.18 m (413.98’) and a depth of approximately 201.62 m (661.5’). 
 
The property is proposed to be severed into three lots - a retained lot, and two new lots 
(shown as Parts 1, 2, and 3 on the survey plan submitted with the application.  Parts 1 and 3 
are the proposed severed lots, while Part 2 will be the retained lot.  The retained lot contains 
an existing dwelling. 
 
All three parcels will be of similar size, with Part 1 being 0.847 ha (2.092 acres), and Part 2 
(the retained parcel) and Part 3 being 0.848 ha (2.095 acres).  Both the retained and 
severed parcels will have frontages of 42.08 m (138.06’), and depths ranging from 201.45 m 
to 201.62 m (660.925’ to 661.483’). 
Conformity with the Official Plan: 
The subject property is designated as Rural Area - Residential in the Prince Township 
Official Plan. The Official Plan states that “new Rural Area-Residential lots shall be 
encouraged to conform to a size consistent with existing lots in the area.” Both the retained 
and severed parcels will conform to the lot area prescribed by the Rural Area - Residential 
policies of the Official Plan. 
 
As noted earlier, the severed and retained parcels will have lot frontages of approximately 
42.08 m (138.06’). The Rural Area - Residential designation of the Official Plan prescribes a 
minimum lot frontage of 45 m. However, the policy prescribing lot area and dimension 
requirements is preceded by a policy direction indicating that “Rural residential lots shall 
have adequate frontage and area to support the intended use, including the provision of 
on-site services and their future replacement in the event of failure.” Both the severed and 
retained parcels will be able to achieve the minimum lot area prescribed by the Rural Area - 
Residential policies of the Official Plan.   
 
While the provided frontages will be slightly less than the prescribed 45 m, the lot sizes 
proposed are consistent with the sizes of existing lots in the area.  For this reason,  it is the 

opinion of the Planner that both the retained and severed parcels conform to the Rural Area 
- Residential policies of the Township’s Official Plan. 
 
As well, as part of the review of this application, correspondence was received from Algoma 
Public Health, who did not indicate any objections or concerns with the proposed severance. 
 
For these reasons, it is the Planner’s opinion that the proposed lot size and dimensions of 
the retained parcel are suitable for the intended rural residential use, and maintains the 
intent of the Rural Area - Residential policies. As such, an amendment to the Official Plan is 
not warranted. 
Conformity with Zoning By-law 2015-19: 
The subject properties are zoned Rural Area - Residential under Zoning By-law 2015-19, 
which requires a minimum lot area of 0.80 ha. Both the retained and severed parcels will 
meet the by-law’s lot area requirements. 
 
To facilitate the severance, both the severed and retained parcels will require variances. The 
severed and retained parcels will require a variance to the frontage, reducing the required lot 
frontage from 45 m (147.6’) to 42.08 m (138.06’). As mentioned above, given the ultimate 
size of the severed and retained parcels and their ability to support the intended rural 
residential use, it is the opinion of the Planner that approval of a variance to the lot frontage 
for the severed and retained parcels is recommended.  
 
The severed and retained parcels will require a variance to the maximum lot depth. The 
maximum required lot depth outlined in the Rural Area - Residential zone standards is 200 m 
(656.168’). If approved, the maximum lot depths for the severed and retained lots will range 
from 201.45 m to 201.62 m (660.925’ to 661.483’). The lot depths are based on the existing 
lot configuration, and are consistent with the abutting Rural Area- Residential lots along 
Base Line. 
 
For this reason, it is the opinion of the Planner that the requested variances are minor in 
nature, desirable for the appropriate development and use of the land, maintain the general 
intent and purpose of the Zoning By-law, and maintain the general intent and purpose of the 
Official Plan. 
Conformity with Provincial Policy: 
Provincial Planning Statement, 2024 Policy 2.5.1 states that “healthy, integrated, and viable 
rural areas should be supported by: a) building upon rural character, and leveraging rural 
amenities and assets.” The proposed variances and consent maintain the rural character of 
the area while ensuring consistency with the Township’s Rural Area land use policies and 
Zoning By-law standards. 
Recommendation: 
RESOLVED THAT the report dated November 3, 2026, regarding Application M2-2026, be 
accepted and that the variances for 2866 Base Line, reducing the required lot frontage from 
45 m (147.6’) to 42.08 m (138.06’), and increasing the maximum lot depth from 200 m 

(656.168’) to between 201.45 m to 201.62 m (660.925’ to 661.483’) for the severed and 
retained parcels, be approved. 
 
Further, that provisional consent for Application C2-2026 be AUTHORIZED for the 
severance and conveyance of an easterly (Part 3) 0.848 ha (2.095 acres) and westerly (Part 
1) 0.847 ha (2.092 acres) parcel of land, both having a lot frontage of approximately 42.08 m 
(138.06’) and a depth of approximately 201.62 m (661.483’) for Part 3, and 201.45 m 
(660.925’) for Part 1, from 2866 Base Line, for the purpose of creating two (2) new lots, 
subject to the following conditions. These conditions must be, in the opinion of the 
Clerk-Treasurer, substantially complied with or waived by the party requesting the condition 
within a two-year period from the date of this decision. Failure to do so will result in consent 
being deemed refused in accordance with Subsection 41 of Section 53 of the Planning Act, 
R.S.O. 1990, as amended. 
 
1.​ TRANSFER/DEED OF LAND: That the Clerk-Treasurer is provided with two (2) 
copies of the “electronic registration in preparation documents”, prior to registration 
on title. The Certificate of Official must be registered with the “electronic registration 
transfer”. NOTE: Any alterations to the legal description after the Clerk-Treasurer has 
issued the Certificate will deem the document(s) invalid and must be returned to the 
Clerk-Treasurer. A new Certificate containing the Registrar-approved legal 
description will have to be endorsed by the Clerk-Treasurer prior to registration of the 
document(s). An issuance fee will apply. 
2.​ REFERENCE PLAN/SURVEY PLAN/LEGAL DESCRIPTION: That the applicant 
provides the Clerk-Treasurer with ten (10) white prints of a deposited plan of 
reference prepared by an Ontario Land Surveyor registered in the Province of 
Ontario and bearing the seal of the Land Registrar depicting the entire land which 
conforms substantially with the application as submitted. At least one copy of a plan 
of reference must illustrate the locations and dimensions of all buildings and 
structures on the subject property and their distance to property lines. 
3.​ PIN ABSTRACT: That a copy of the latest PIN abstract accompanies the “electronic 
registration in preparation documents”. 
4.​ SEPTIC SYSTEM: A letter from Algoma Public Health must be submitted to the 
Clerk-Treasurer indicating that an on-site septic system can be supported on the 
severed lot, if new development is proposed. 
5.​ ON-SITE PROVEN WELL: Confirmation of an established on-site proven well must 
be submitted to the Clerk-Treasurer prior to final consent, if new development is 
proposed. 
6.​ DISCHARGE OF CHARGE/MORTGAGE: That any Charge/Mortgage affecting the 
subject property is discharged and a copy of the Discharge of Charge/Mortgage be 
provided to the Clerk-Treasurer or written acknowledgement that the holder of the 
Charge/Mortgage is satisfied with the Consent being finalised. 
7.​ PARTIAL DISCHARGE OF CHARGE/MORTGAGE: That the Clerk-Treasurer is 
provided with two (2) copies of the Discharge of Charge/Mortgage documents for the 
parcel for which the Consent relates to and the Discharge refers to for certification. 
Certificate of Official must be registered with the Discharge of Charge/Mortgage 
documents. A Solicitor’s undertaking must be provided to the Clerk-Treasurer 
undertaking to register the Discharge and to provide the Clerk Treasurer with a copy 
of the registered documents. 

8.​ PAYMENT OF MUNICIPAL TAXES: Prior to final approval, the owner provides to the 
Clerk-Treasurer confirmation of payment of all outstanding taxes, including penalties 
and interest (and any local improvement charges, if applicable). 
9.​ CASH-IN-LIEU OF PARKLAND: Prior to final approval, the owner shall provide 
payment to the Township of cash-in-lieu of parkland dedication, in accordance with 
Section 51.1 of the Planning Act, R.S.O. 1990, as amended, and any applicable 
Township by-law. 
10.​SOLICITOR'S UNDERTAKING: Prior to final approval, the applicant's solicitor shall 
provide a written undertaking to the Clerk-Treasurer to register the necessary transfer 
documents within two years of the issuance of the Certificate of Official, 
acknowledging that failure to register within this period will result in the consent 
lapsing. 
11.​RECEIPTED 
ELECTRONIC 
TRANSFER: The applicant must provide the 
Clerk-Treasurer with a copy of the Receipted Electronic Transfer upon registration of 
the Transfer and prior to the issuance of any Building Permits. 
12.​THIS provisional approval does not become effective until the above conditions have 
been satisfied. ALL conditions must be fulfilled within two years of the date of the 
giving of the Notice of Decision, failing which this application shall thereupon be 
deemed to be refused pursuant to Section 53(41) of the Planning Act, R.S.O. 1990, 
as amended. 
 

 
 
