CORPORATION OF THE TOWNSHIP OF PRINCE 
 
Prince Township Clerk’s Council Report: Oct 2026 
Note: 
   Regular Council Meeting 
Subject:                                 Clerk’s Report on Ongoing projects and Updates 
Prepared by: 
 
     Megha Trivedi, Clerk - Treasurer 
Purpose 
The purpose of this report is to provide Council with an update on current administrative matters, 
projects, and activities undertaken since the previous regular meeting of Council. 
 
1. 2026 Municipal Election 
Administration continues preparations for the 2026 Municipal Election, with Advance Voting Day 
scheduled for October 14, 2026, and Voting Day scheduled for October 26, 2026. 
Election preparations are nearing completion and have included finalization of voting procedures 
and statutory forms, election worker arrangements and training, voting place materials, ballot 
security and counting procedures, and ongoing revisions to the Voters' List. 
Administration will continue completing the remaining statutory and operational requirements 
leading up to Advance Voting Day and Voting Day. 
2. Highway 550 – Ministry of Transportation 
Administration has continued correspondence with the Ministry of Transportation regarding 
concerns associated with Highway 550. 
The Ministry has completed its review of the corridor and has identified potential improvements, 
including the establishment of a 70 km/h transition zone, removal of certain passing opportunities, 
and improvements to signage. 
The proposed changes are currently proceeding through the Provincial review and approval 
process. Based on the most recent update received from the Ministry, implementation of the 
proposed improvements is anticipated in 2027, subject to completion of the Provincial review 
process. 
 
3. Animal Control and By-law Enforcement  
Administration continues to address ongoing animal control and by-law enforcement matters 

within the Township. Staff continue to respond to complaints, conduct follow-up as required, and 
coordinate enforcement activities in accordance with the Township's applicable by-laws. 
4. Noise Control By-law Review 
 
Through the review of current enforcement matters, administration has also identified the need to 
revisit the Township's existing Noise Control By-law. The Township's current Noise Control By-law 
dates from 1991 and was previously brought before Council for consideration of an update. 
A draft updated Noise Control By-law was subsequently prepared based on Council's previous 
recommendations. The draft by-law is included later in the Council agenda package for Council's 
review and consideration. 
5. Accounts Payable – Direct Deposit Implementation 
Administration has implemented direct deposit/electronic payment capabilities for accounts 
payable as part of ongoing efforts to modernize the Township's financial processes and improve 
administrative efficiency. 
Administration will begin contacting Township vendors to obtain the required banking information 
and authorization to allow future payments to be deposited directly into vendors' bank accounts. 
The objective is to transition vendors to electronic payments where possible and minimize the 
Township's reliance on issuing and processing cheques. 
The implementation is expected to improve payment efficiency, reduce cheque processing and 
mailing requirements, and provide a more streamlined accounts payable process for both the 
Township and its vendors. 
6. Deans Road Park – Project Update 
The improvement work at Deans Road Park has reached substantial completion. 
The gate installation has been completed, with the fencing installation remaining to be completed. 
Administration will continue to monitor the remaining work through final completion of the project. 
 
 
Submitted respectfully, 
Megha Trivedi 
Clerk-Treasurer 

Township of Prince | 2026 Asset Management Annual Review 
Township of Prince - 2026 Asset Management Annual Review 
TOWNSHIP OF PRINCE 
2026 ASSET MANAGEMENT ANNUAL REVIEW 
Ontario Regulation 588/17 
To: 
Mayor and Members of Council 
From: 
Clerk-Treasurer 
Date: 
October 13, 2026 
Subject: 
2026 Asset Management Annual Review - O. Reg. 588/17 
Reporting Period: 
September 17, 2025 to September 30, 2026 
 
RECOMMENDATION 
 
THAT Council, having reviewed the 2026 Asset Management Annual Review, hereby conducts the annual 
review required by section 9 of Ontario Regulation 588/17 and receives the report as presented; 
AND THAT Council acknowledges the progress made in implementing the Township of Prince 2025 Asset 
Management Plan, the factors affecting implementation, and the strategies identified in this report; 
AND THAT Administration be directed to incorporate outstanding asset-management priorities into preparation 
of the 2027 operating and capital budgets and the Township's long-term financial planning; and 
AND THAT Administration schedule future annual asset-management reviews for Council consideration on or 
before July 1 each year. 
COVER & CONTEXT 
 
Purpose of the Annual Review 
This report is the Township of Prince's annual review of asset-management progress. It is structured using the Asset 
Management Annual Review Template provided for Ontario municipalities and addresses the three matters required by 
section 9(2) of Ontario Regulation 588/17: progress implementing the Asset Management Plan (AMP), factors impeding 
implementation, and a strategy to address those factors. 
Approved Asset Management Plan / Council Reference 
The plan under review is the Township of Prince 2025 Asset Management Plan prepared by BDO Canada LLP. At its 
September 17, 2025 meeting, Council adopted Resolution 2025-147, receiving the Asset Management Plan presentation 
and directing that the plan be used to guide future infrastructure investment and financial planning. 
Legislative and Policy Context 
Ontario Regulation 588/17, made under the Infrastructure for Jobs and Prosperity Act, 2015, requires every municipal 
council to conduct an annual review of its asset-management progress on or before July 1 each year, beginning in the 
year after completion of the municipality's Phase 4 AMP. The regulation also requires the review to address 
implementation progress, impediments, and a strategy to address those impediments. 
The Township's Strategic Asset Management Policy is established through By-law 2023-32. The current 2025 AMP is 
publicly posted on the Township's website. The Municipal Act, 2001 also provides the broader governance and financial-
accountability framework: section 224 assigns Council responsibility for administrative and controllership practices and for 
maintaining the municipality's financial integrity, while section 286 assigns the Treasurer responsibility for the 
municipality's financial affairs and for providing Council with required financial information. 
Compliance Statement 
This review has been prepared to satisfy the substantive annual-review requirements in section 9(2) of O. Reg. 588/17. 
The regulation requires the annual review to be conducted on or before July 1. The Township's 2026 review is being 
brought forward after that date. Administration will incorporate the annual review into its recurring work plan so that future 
reviews are presented to Council on or before July 1. 

Township of Prince | 2026 Asset Management Annual Review 
Township of Prince - 2026 Asset Management Annual Review 
1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY 
 
• The 2025 AMP remains the Township's current long-term framework for asset condition, levels of service, lifecycle needs, 
risk and financing priorities. 
• The Gagnon Road Bridge, identified in the 2025 AMP as being in poor condition and requiring immediate action, was 
replaced through construction carried out in 2025 and 2026. 
• In 2026, the Township applied for NORDS funding toward rehabilitation of the Municipal Building, with the application 
focused on roof, window and exterior facade work. 
• No additional AMP-related capital project completions have been confirmed for this review period. Remaining priorities 
continue within the AMP's multi-year planning horizons. 
• The completed Gagnon Road Bridge represents an improvement in the risk profile and reliability of the Township's bridge 
infrastructure. No other material change in levels of service has been documented through this review. 
• The Township will continue to use risk-based prioritization, asset condition, available funding and Council budget decisions 
to sequence capital work. 
• The 2027 budget process should incorporate the outstanding AMP priorities and update asset records for completed capital 
work, including the Gagnon Road Bridge. 
• Future annual reviews will be scheduled for Council consideration on or before July 1. 
2. CAPITAL DELIVERY REVIEW 
 
The template recommends reviewing significant changes to the asset base and comparing planned work against 
delivered or deferred work. The following table summarizes the significant AMP items for the reporting period. 
