Mr. Dedrick advised Council that DesignPoint Engineering & Surveying Ltd. completed assessment and 
structural evaluation of the Roseway River Trail Bridge and the Tom Tigney Trail Bridge to establish the 
existing bridge conditions and suitability for public use including pedestrian, bicycle, and off highway 
vehicle (OHV) traffic. The final reports will be shared once received. 
Mr. Dedrick advised Council that both bridges are at the end of their lifespans and will require 
rehabilitation or replacement and noted their importance within the trail system with connectivity being 
considered. Unlike the Roseway River Trail Bridge, the Tom Tigney Trail Bridge does not have an alternate 
route. Council must also consider the potential cost of replacing both bridges, to continue supporting 
connectivity, especially for OHVs. 
Mr. Dedrick also provided the scenario of terminating the Letter of Authority (LOA) with the Department 
of Natural Resources and Renewal (DNRR) for Council to consider. If the LOA for Roseway River Trail 
were to be terminated, the Municipality would no longer be responsible for maintaining the bridge, it 
would transfer back to DNRR. This would allow the Municipality to allocate resources towards the Tom 
Tigney Bridge due to its location and importance within the trail system. 
A discussion was held regarding potential cost estimates of replacing both bridges, liability, funding 
options, the LOA termination period, and continued collaboration with Woodland Multi-use Trail 
Association. 
