Sundridge-Strong Union Public Library Board Policy Manual 
 
 
PROCUREMENT 
Adopted: ~1990 
Revised:  June 2020, October 2026 
 
Reviewed:    
 
The Sundridge-Strong Union Public Library Board (the library) recognizes the 
procurement of goods and services must be undertaken in a way that complies with 
all applicable legislative requirements and provides the best value for the library 
while balancing quality, cost, and effective and efficient processes. 
Section 270 (2) of the Municipal Act S.O. 2001, Chapter 25, as amended requires 
local boards to adopt and maintain policies with respect to the procurement of 
goods and services. 
 
Purpose 
 
• 
To maintain the integrity of the procurement process by ensuring that, 
wherever possible, competitive methods of procurement are used to obtain the 
best value. 
 
• 
To obtain the highest quality goods or services at the least possible cost, while 
at the same time weighing the life cycle costs and environmental impacts of the 
goods and services being purchased. 
 
• 
To ensure fairness, objectivity, and transparency in the procurement process. 
 
• 
To incorporate sustainability into the procurement process where appropriate 
and feasible. 
 
• 
To clearly define the circumstances in which non-competitive procurements are 
awarded and disposal of goods procured.  
 
Scope 
 
This policy covers the procurement of all goods and services by the library. This 
includes, but is not limited to: 
 
• 
Goods: collections, collections processing supplies, computer hardware and 
software, furniture and equipment, office supplies, construction and building 
materials. 
 
• 
Professional Services: financial, consulting, engineering, information 
technology, human resource management, and security. 
 
• 
Facility Services: construction, cleaning, HVAC maintenance, landscaping, and 
snow removal services. 

Procurement - Page 2 of 4 
 
Definitions 
 
• 
Best value: the balance of quality and performance including delivery precision 
and cost using a pre-determined evaluation plan. 
 
• 
Bid: an offer or submission received from a vendor in response to a request. 
 
• 
Bidder: a person, company, or organization that submits a bid in response to 
any type of procurement process. 
 
• 
Contract: a binding agreement between at least two parties. In some cases, a 
purchase order constitutes a contract. 
 
• 
Procurement: the acquisition of goods and/or services required to provide the 
services and execute the operations of the library. 
 
• 
Supplier: an individual or organization providing goods or services to the library 
such as a contractor, service organization, vendor, or consultant. 
 
Policy Objectives 
 
The Sundridge-Strong Union Public Library will be led by these guiding principles: 
 
• 
The library endeavours to procure goods and services from ethical, responsible 
suppliers were possible. 
 
• 
The library endeavours to procure goods and services from Ontarian or 
Canadian suppliers where possible. (See the Municipal Buy Ontario Procurement 
Directive at https://www.ontario.ca/page/municipal-buy-ontario-procurement-
directive). 
 
• 
The library endeavours to procure goods and services while respecting the 
preservation of the natural environment and, where feasible, encourage 
suppliers to supply “green” products. 
 
• 
Purchases are made in compliance with all relevant statutes and regulations.  
 
• 
Procurement decisions follow an open, accountable, fair, and efficient process. 
This includes standardized procedures, communicating with bidders, evaluating 
submissions, awarding contracts, and maintaining records. 
 
Responsibility 
 
The Sundridge-Strong Union Public Library Board authorizes the CEO/Librarian to 
establish procurement procedures required to enact this policy.   
 

Procurement - Page 3 of 4 
 
• 
The CEO /Librarian is authorized to proceed with formal agreements and 
contracts on behalf of the library. 
 
• 
The CEO/Librarian is authorized to delegate authority for procurement, 
depending on the scope and value of a purchase or contract (see Appendix). 
 
• 
The authority to proceed with procurement is subject to the availability of 
sufficient funds within the approved library budget. If a capital project exceeds, 
or is anticipated to exceed, the approved contingency threshold, the board must 
be notified to approve additional funds.  
 
Conflict of Interest 
 
A member of the library board or an employee of the library must declare pecuniary 
interest in any proposal, contract, tender, or quotation for the supply of goods and 
services to the library, should they have direct or indirect interest in said work or 
project. Members of the library board are subject to the requirements of the 
Municipal Conflict of Interest Act. 
 
Exclusions 
 
This policy includes the purchase of all goods and services for the library except for 
the following: 
 
• 
Utilities such as water, electricity, and gas. 
 
• 
Training and education such as conferences, registration, courses, and 
workshops. 
 
• 
Refundable employee expenses such as travel, mileage, and accommodation. 
 
• 
General expenses such as licenses and postage. 
 
• 
Petty cash items less than $100. 
 
Disposal of Materials and Goods Procured 
 
The CEO/Librarian is responsible for the handling of surplus of all library materials 
and shall determine the appropriate method of conveyance to best meet the 
library’s needs. 
 
Arrangements are made for the disposal of surplus goods and materials in any way 
that is believed will provide the highest return to the library including, but not 
limited to: 
 
• 
Trade-in as part of the procurement of other similar goods acquired by the 
library. 
 

Procurement - Page 4 of 4 
 
• 
Donation to another community organization or neighbouring library. 
 
• 
Sale for a nominal fee. 
 
• 
Public auction. 
 
• 
Classifying as waste and recycling, dismantling, destroying and/or disposing. 
 
Appendix: Spending Authority 
 
Type of 
procurement 
Authority 
Single source 
The CEO approves purchase of everyday items up to a 
value of $2,000. 
 
Request for 
quotation (RFQ) 
Goods and services between $2,500 and $20,000 require 
two (2) written quotes, and between $20,001 and 
$50,000 require three (3) written quotes.   
 
The CEO or designate approves contracts up to $20,000. 
Contracts over $20,000 are awarded based on approval of 
the library board. 
 
Request for proposal 
(RFP) 
RFPs are formal invitations to suppliers to identify specific 
services, equipment or products which would meet the 
requirements of the library. 
 
The library requirements are outlined in a general 
performance specification document and all proposals 
evaluated against clearly stated criteria and specifications. 
 
The selection is not made solely based on the lowest 
dollar value that meets the requirement of the proposal. 
 
The CEO or designate approves contracts up to $20,000. 
Contracts over $20,000 are awarded based on approval of 
the library board. 
 
 

